const DECKS = [];

/* ================= Inquiry Question 8.1: Origins of the Elements ================= */
DECKS.push({ id: 'phys-m8-bb', fa: 'fa1', title: 'The Big Bang: radiation into matter', dps: ['8.1.1'], cards: [
  { id: 'p8bb01', dp: '8.1.1', t: 'def', q: 'How does NESA define a scientific <b>theory</b>, and what is the Big Bang theory?',
    a: P('<b>Theory</b>: a set of concepts, claims and/or laws that can be used to explain and predict a wide range of related observed phenomena. Theories are founded on clearly identified assumptions, are testable, produce reproducible results and have explanatory power.') + P('<b>Big Bang theory</b> (first proposed by Georges Lemaître, 1927): the Universe began as an enormous amount of energy at a single point (a <b>singularity</b>), and all matter condensed from this energy as space expanded and cooled.') + N('A theory can be changed when new experimental evidence cannot be explained by it.') },
  { id: 'p8bb02', dp: '8.1.1', t: 'con', q: 'Why do physicists say the Big Bang was a rapid expansion, not an explosion?',
    a: L(['An explosion throws pieces outwards into <b>existing</b> surrounding space.', 'The Big Bang was the start of the <b>expansion (stretching) of space itself</b> from a very hot, compressed beginning.', 'There was no ‘outside’ for matter to fly into, and no centre: every part of space expands away from every other part.']) },
  { id: 'p8bb03', dp: '8.1.1', t: 'rec', q: 'Summarise the main ideas of the Big Bang theory in five points.',
    a: L(['The Universe began compressed into a <b>singularity</b> and has been expanding ever since (about 13.8 billion years).', 'It began as pure <b>energy (radiation)</b> at an estimated 10^{32} K, far too hot for matter.', 'As it expanded it <b>cooled</b>; energy converted into matter (E = mc^2): first fundamental particles (quarks, electrons), then hadrons (protons, neutrons), then light nuclei and atoms (H, He).', '<b>Gravity</b> pulled matter together into clouds, galaxies and stars.', 'The Universe is still expanding and cooling.']) },
  { id: 'p8bb04', dp: '8.1.1', t: 'rec', q: 'List, in order, the six processes that transformed radiation into matter after the Big Bang.',
    a: OL(['<b>Inflation</b>', '<b>Pair production</b>', '<b>Annihilation</b>', '<b>Big Bang nucleosynthesis</b>', '<b>Recombination</b>', '<b>Decoupling</b>']) + N('Your booklet marks this list as important: learn the order.'), v: 'p8bbt' },
  { id: 'p8bb05', dp: '8.1.1', t: 'con', q: 'Explain <b>pair production</b> and <b>annihilation</b> in the early Universe.',
    a: L(['<b>Pair production</b>: two extremely high-energy gamma photons collide and their energy becomes the mass of a <b>particle–antiparticle pair</b> (e.g. an electron and a positron), according to E = Δmc^2.', '<b>Annihilation</b>: when a particle meets its antiparticle, all their mass is converted back into energy as high-energy <b>gamma photons</b>.', 'These photons could form new pairs, so the two processes went back and forth.', 'As the temperature fell, photons had less energy, so only lighter particle pairs could form (e.g. electrons and positrons, neutrinos).']) },
  { id: 'p8bb06', dp: '8.1.1', t: 'con', q: 'Describe how matter formed as the Universe cooled, from the elementary particle era to recombination.',
    a: OL(['<b>Elementary particle (quark) era</b>: leptons (electrons, muons, taus, neutrinos) and quarks, plus their antiparticles, form, along with gluons and photons.', '<b>Hadron era</b>: quarks combine into hadrons such as <b>protons and neutrons</b>; still far too hot for atoms.', '<b>Big Bang nucleosynthesis</b> (about 100 s, 10^9 K): protons and neutrons fuse into deuterium, then helium, plus a trace of lithium-7.', '<b>Recombination</b> (about 300 000 years): the Universe is cool enough for electrons to join nuclei, forming neutral hydrogen and helium atoms.', '<b>Decoupling</b>: photons can now travel freely, so the Universe becomes transparent.']) },
  { id: 'p8bb07', dp: '8.1.1', t: 'con', q: 'Why could only the lightest elements form in Big Bang nucleosynthesis?',
    a: L(['Fusing nuclei requires overcoming the <b>electrostatic repulsion</b> between positive nuclei; the more protons, the harder this is.', 'The Universe was expanding and <b>cooling rapidly</b>, so the window of high enough temperature and density was only minutes long.', 'So only H, deuterium, He and a trace of Li formed. Heavier elements are made later by <b>nucleosynthesis in stars and supernovae</b>.']) },
  { id: 'p8bb08', dp: '8.1.1', t: 'con', q: 'What is <b>decoupling</b>, and how is it linked to the cosmic microwave background radiation (CMBR)?',
    a: L(['Before recombination, photons kept scattering off free electrons and charged nuclei, so the Universe was opaque.', 'Once neutral atoms formed, photons could travel freely: radiation <b>decoupled</b> from matter and the Universe became transparent.', 'Those photons have travelled through space ever since. The expansion of the Universe has stretched their wavelengths into the microwave region: this is the <b>CMBR</b>.']) },
  { id: 'p8bb09', dp: '8.1.1', t: 'con', q: 'Outline how galaxies and stars formed after the Big Bang.',
    a: L(['Slightly more matter than antimatter formed; after annihilation the leftover matter was unevenly spread, so the Universe was <b>‘lumpy’</b>.', 'Denser regions exerted greater <b>gravitational attraction</b>, pulling in matter to form protogalaxies (from about 1 million to 1 billion years).', 'As clouds collapsed their <b>rotation sped up</b> (like a spinning skater pulling in their arms), flattening them into discs, which is why most galaxies are flattened spirals.', 'Within the clouds, clumps collapsed further, becoming compressed and hotter until the cores were hot enough to sustain <b>nuclear fusion</b>: stars formed.']) },
  { id: 'p8bb10', dp: '8.1.1', t: 'exam', q: 'Long response (4 marks): Critically analyse the statement: “Matter as we know it today was formed by a huge explosion in space known as the Big Bang.”',
    a: L(['<b>Inaccurate wording</b>: the Big Bang was not an explosion <b>in</b> space; it was the rapid expansion of space itself from a hot, dense singularity.', '<b>Partly true</b>: the energy of the Big Bang is the source of all matter, through E = mc^2 (pair production as the Universe cooled).', '<b>Incomplete</b>: matter did not form at once. Quarks and leptons formed first, then protons and neutrons, then only H, He and a trace of Li by Big Bang nucleosynthesis, and atoms only after about 300 000 years (recombination).', '<b>Heavier elements</b> (e.g. carbon, oxygen, iron, gold) formed much later in stars and supernovae, so most ‘matter as we know it’ was not formed in the Big Bang itself.']) }
]});

DECKS.push({ id: 'phys-m8-hu', fa: 'fa1', title: 'Evidence for the expanding Universe: Hubble', dps: ['8.1.2'], cards: [
  { id: 'p8hu01', dp: '8.1.2', t: 'def', q: 'What is the <b>Doppler effect</b>? Use a passing ambulance as an example.',
    a: P('The apparent change in frequency (and wavelength) of a wave when there is relative motion between the source and the observer.') + L(['As the ambulance approaches, the waves are squashed together: <b>shorter wavelength, higher frequency</b>, higher pitch.', 'As it moves away, the waves are stretched: <b>longer wavelength, lower frequency</b>, lower pitch.']) },
  { id: 'p8hu02', dp: '8.1.2', t: 'con', q: 'What do <b>red shift</b> and <b>blue shift</b> of spectral lines tell astronomers?',
    a: L(['Each element’s spectral lines have known laboratory wavelengths.', '<b>Red shift</b>: lines appear at longer wavelengths (towards the red end), so the object is <b>moving away</b> from us.', '<b>Blue shift</b>: lines appear at shorter wavelengths (towards the blue end), so the object is <b>moving towards</b> us.', 'The <b>size</b> of the shift gives the speed: the bigger the shift, the faster the relative motion.']) + N('The objects do not look red or blue; it is their spectral lines that shift.') },
  { id: 'p8hu03', dp: '8.1.2', t: 'con', q: 'What did <b>Henrietta Leavitt</b> (1912) and <b>Vesto Slipher</b> (1912) each discover?',
    a: L(['<b>Leavitt</b>: Cepheid variable stars brighten and dim in a regular pattern, and their pulsation period is linked to their true brightness. Comparing true brightness with how bright they appear gives their <b>distance</b>: the first ‘standard candle’ for measuring distances to galaxies.', '<b>Slipher</b>: the spectra of spiral nebulae (galaxies) were almost all <b>red-shifted</b>, so they were <b>moving away</b> from us; the size of the shift gave their speed.']) },
  { id: 'p8hu04', dp: '8.1.2', t: 'con', q: 'What did <b>Edwin Hubble</b> discover, and how?',
    a: L(['1924: using a Cepheid variable in the Andromeda nebula, he showed it was far outside the Milky Way, so it was a <b>separate galaxy</b>. The Universe was far bigger than the Milky Way.', 'He measured distances to many galaxies with <b>Leavitt’s Cepheid method</b> and their speeds from <b>Slipher-style red shifts</b>.', 'He found nearly all galaxies are moving away, and <b>the further away a galaxy is, the faster it recedes</b>: v ∝ D (Hubble’s law, or the Hubble–Lemaître law).', 'This is evidence that the <b>Universe is expanding</b>.']) + N('Your booklet dates Hubble’s red-shift work to 1927; his law was published in 1929.') },
  { id: 'p8hu05', dp: '8.1.2', t: 'eq', q: 'State <b>Hubble’s law</b>, with units, and the approximate value of the Hubble constant.',
    a: E('v = H_0D') + L(['v = recession velocity of the galaxy (km s^{-1})', 'D = distance to the galaxy (megaparsecs, Mpc)', 'H_0 = Hubble constant ≈ 70 km s^{-1} Mpc^{-1} (the gradient of a v–D graph)']) + N('Not on your data sheet. 1 parsec ≈ 3.26 light years = 3.086 × 10^{16} m, so 1 Mpc = 3.086 × 10^{22} m.') },
  { id: 'p8hu06', dp: '8.1.2', t: 'calc', q: 'Show that the age of the Universe is t = 1/H_0, and calculate it for H_0 = 72.1 km s^{-1} Mpc^{-1}.',
    a: P('Distance = speed × time, so D = vt. Hubble’s law gives D = ' + F('v', 'H_0') + '. Equating: vt = ' + F('v', 'H_0') + ', so:') + E('t = ' + F('1', 'H_0')) + OL(['H_0 = ' + F('72.1 × 10^3 m s^{-1}', '3.086 × 10^{22} m') + ' = 2.336 × 10^{-18} s^{-1}', 't = 1 ÷ 2.336 × 10^{-18} = 4.28 × 10^{17} s', 't = 4.28 × 10^{17} ÷ (365.25 × 24 × 3600) = <b>1.36 × 10^{10} years</b> (about 13.6 billion years)']) + N('Convert km to m and Mpc to m first, so H_0 is in s^{-1}.') },
  { id: 'p8hu07', dp: '8.1.2', t: 'calc', q: 'Students plot Hubble data and draw a line of best fit through the origin and the point (196 Mpc, 13 000 km s^{-1}). Find H_0 and the age of the Universe.',
    a: OL(['Gradient = H_0 = ' + F('13 000', '196') + ' = <b>66 km s^{-1} Mpc^{-1}</b>', 'In SI units: ' + F('13 000 × 10^3 m s^{-1}', '196 × 3.086 × 10^{22} m') + ' = 2.149 × 10^{-18} s^{-1}', 't = 1/H_0 = 4.65 × 10^{17} s ÷ 3.156 × 10^7 s per year = <b>1.47 × 10^{10} years</b> (14.7 billion)']) + N('From your booklet’s skills worksheet. The result is close to the accepted age because data scatter and older H_0 values differ.') },
  { id: 'p8hu08', dp: '8.1.2', t: 'exam', q: 'Assess the importance of Leavitt’s and Slipher’s discoveries to Hubble’s discovery.',
    a: L(['Hubble’s law relates two quantities, <b>distance</b> and <b>velocity</b>, and he needed a way to measure each.', '<b>Leavitt</b>’s Cepheid period–brightness relationship gave the <b>distances</b> to galaxies.', '<b>Slipher</b>’s red-shift measurements gave their <b>recession velocities</b>.', '<b>Judgement</b>: both were essential. Without either, Hubble could not have found that v ∝ D, the key evidence that the Universe is expanding.']) },
  { id: 'p8hu09', dp: '8.1.2', t: 'rec', q: 'Describe the three main pieces of experimental evidence for the Big Bang theory.',
    a: OL(['<b>Red shift of distant galaxies</b> (Slipher, Hubble): galaxies in every direction are receding, faster the further away (v = H_0D), so space is expanding, as the theory predicts.', '<b>Hydrogen–helium ratio</b>: the Universe is about 75% hydrogen and 25% helium by mass (3 : 1), matching the ratio predicted for Big Bang nucleosynthesis.', '<b>Cosmic microwave background radiation</b>: predicted by Gamow (1948), found by Penzias and Wilson (1964); black-body radiation at about 2.7 K, almost the same in every direction.']) + N('Extension evidence in your booklet: too few hot stars to have made all the helium; distant (young) galaxies differ from nearby ones; particle physics and cosmology agree.') },
  { id: 'p8hu10', dp: '8.1.2', t: 'con', q: 'Explain why the Big Bang theory predicts a hydrogen : helium mass ratio of about 3 : 1.',
    a: L(['As the early Universe cooled, neutron production stopped, leaving about <b>7 protons for every neutron</b> (14 protons : 2 neutrons).', 'The 2 neutrons combine with 2 protons to make <b>one helium-4 nucleus</b>; the other 12 protons become <b>12 hydrogen atoms</b>.', 'By mass: helium 4 units, hydrogen 12 units, so <b>H : He = 3 : 1</b> (75% H, 25% He).', 'This matches the observed abundance, supporting the theory.']) + N('By number of atoms it is about 12 : 1; the 3 : 1 is by mass. (One extension box in your booklet says ‘3 hydrogen atoms to every 1 helium atom’; it should say ‘by mass’.)') },
  { id: 'p8hu11', dp: '8.1.2', t: 'con', q: 'Explain how the cosmic microwave background radiation supports the Big Bang theory.',
    a: L(['If the Universe began hot and dense, the radiation from that time should still fill space, stretched by expansion to long wavelengths (about 1 mm, microwaves).', 'Gamow predicted this in 1948; Penzias and Wilson detected it by accident in 1964 (Nobel Prize 1978).', 'COBE and later Planck (2013) showed it is a near-perfect <b>black-body spectrum</b> peaking at about 1.1 mm, corresponding to about <b>2.7 K</b>.', 'It is almost identical in every direction (variations of about 1 part in 100 000), as expected for radiation left over from the early Universe.']) },
  { id: 'p8hu12', dp: '8.1.2', t: 'con', q: 'Why did Einstein add a ‘cosmological constant’ to his equations?',
    a: L(['His general theory of relativity implied the Universe should be <b>expanding</b> (or contracting).', 'Einstein believed the Universe was <b>static</b>, so he added a cosmological constant to force his equations to give an unchanging Universe.', 'Hubble’s evidence of expansion later showed the Universe is not static.']) }
]});

DECKS.push({ id: 'phys-m8-sp', fa: 'fa1', title: 'Emission, absorption and black body spectra', dps: ['8.1.4'], cards: [
  { id: 'p8sp01', dp: '8.1.4', t: 'rec', q: 'Name the three types of spectra and how each is produced.',
    a: L(['<b>Continuous</b>: all wavelengths (a full rainbow) from a hot solid, liquid or high-pressure gas, e.g. an incandescent filament or the dense interior of a star.', '<b>Emission</b>: bright coloured lines on a dark background, from a hot, low-pressure gas (e.g. a gas discharge tube).', '<b>Absorption</b>: a continuous spectrum crossed by dark lines, produced when continuous light passes through a cooler, low-pressure gas.']), v: 'p8spectra' },
  { id: 'p8sp02', dp: '8.1.4', t: 'con', q: 'Use the Bohr model to explain how an <b>emission spectrum</b> is produced.',
    a: OL(['Energy (electrical or heat) is supplied to a hot, low-pressure gas and <b>absorbed by electrons</b>, which jump from the ground state to <b>higher energy levels</b>.', 'The excited electrons fall back to lower levels and, by <b>conservation of energy</b>, each emits a photon carrying the exact energy difference: ΔE = E_{i} − E_{f} = hf = ' + F('hc', 'λ') + '.', 'Because the energy levels are fixed, only <b>specific wavelengths</b> are emitted: bright lines on a dark background.', 'Each element has different energy levels, so a <b>unique</b> set of lines that identifies it.']) },
  { id: 'p8sp03', dp: '8.1.4', t: 'con', q: 'Explain how an <b>absorption spectrum</b> forms, and why its dark lines match the emission lines of the same element.',
    a: L(['Continuous light passes through a cooler gas. Atoms absorb only photons whose energy exactly equals a possible electron transition (ΔE = hf) to a higher level.', 'The excited electrons fall back and <b>re-emit</b> those photons, but <b>in all directions</b>, so much less of that wavelength continues in the original direction: a dark line.', 'The same energy-level gaps cause both absorption and emission, so the dark lines are at <b>exactly the same wavelengths</b> as the element’s emission lines.']) },
  { id: 'p8sp04', dp: '8.1.4', t: 'def', q: 'What is a <b>black body</b>, and what are the features of its spectrum?',
    a: P('A <b>black body</b> absorbs all radiation that falls on it and re-emits it as a continuous spectrum that depends only on its temperature. Hot filaments and stars approximate black bodies.') + L(['A <b>continuous</b> spread of wavelengths, with intensity varying smoothly.', 'A peak intensity at λ_{max}.', 'As temperature increases, λ_{max} moves to <b>shorter</b> wavelengths and the total intensity increases.']) },
  { id: 'p8sp05', dp: '8.1.4', t: 'eq', q: 'State <b>Wien’s law</b> and what each symbol means.',
    a: E('λ_{max} = ' + F('b', 'T')) + L(['λ_{max} = wavelength of maximum intensity (m)', 'T = surface temperature (K)', 'b = Wien’s displacement constant = 2.898 × 10^{-3} m K (on your data sheet)']) + N('Convert nm to m first. A hotter star peaks at a shorter wavelength, so it looks bluer.') },
  { id: 'p8sp06', dp: '8.1.4', t: 'calc', q: 'A star’s spectrum peaks at 458.0 nm. Calculate its surface temperature.',
    a: E('T = ' + F('b', 'λ_{max}') + ' = ' + F('2.898 × 10^{-3}', '458.0 × 10^{-9}')) + P('<b>T = 6.33 × 10^3 K</b> (about 6300 K)') + N('Real stellar spectra are approximate black bodies with absorption dips, so read the peak of the overall curve.') },
  { id: 'p8sp07', dp: '8.1.4', t: 'con', q: 'Compare emission and absorption spectra with a continuous black body spectrum.',
    a: L(['<b>Similarity</b>: all three are produced by electromagnetic radiation from matter, and a spectroscope can display each.', '<b>Black body</b>: continuous (every wavelength), from a hot dense object; its shape depends only on <b>temperature</b>.', '<b>Emission</b>: only discrete wavelengths (bright lines) from a hot low-pressure gas; the pattern depends on the <b>element</b>.', '<b>Absorption</b>: a continuous spectrum with discrete wavelengths missing (dark lines), where a cooler gas sits in front of a hot source; the missing lines identify the element.']) },
  { id: 'p8sp08', dp: '8.1.4', t: 'con', q: 'Why does each element have a unique emission (or absorption) spectrum?',
    a: L(['Each element has a different nuclear charge (number of protons) and number of electrons.', 'This changes the forces holding the electrons and so the <b>energy levels</b> they can occupy.', 'So the energy differences between levels, and therefore the wavelengths emitted or absorbed (ΔE = hc/λ), are different for every element: a unique ‘fingerprint’.']) },
  { id: 'p8sp09', dp: '8.1.4', t: 'con', q: 'The Sun’s continuous spectrum has dark lines at the hydrogen wavelengths. Account for these lines.',
    a: L(['The hot, dense interior of the Sun emits a near <b>continuous (black body)</b> spectrum.', 'Cooler, less dense gas in the Sun’s <b>outer layers</b> (and atoms along the path to us) absorbs photons whose energies match hydrogen’s electron transitions.', 'These are re-emitted in all directions, so those wavelengths are dimmer in the light reaching us: dark <b>absorption lines</b> at hydrogen’s wavelengths.']) },
  { id: 'p8sp10', dp: '8.1.4', t: 'prac', q: 'Describe a practical for observing different types of spectra, and its main limitation.',
    a: L(['Look through a hand-held spectroscope (a diffraction grating) at: gas discharge tubes (low-pressure gas with a high DC voltage), an incandescent filament globe, and reflected sunlight.', '<b>Results</b>: discharge tubes give emission line spectra; the filament gives a continuous spectrum; sunlight is continuous with absorption lines.', '<b>Limitation</b>: a simple spectroscope is not sensitive or finely focused enough to show the dark absorption lines in sunlight clearly, so validity for that source is questionable.']) + N('Never look directly at the Sun: view reflected sunlight only.') }
]});

DECKS.push({ id: 'phys-m8-st', fa: 'fa1', title: 'Stellar spectra and classifying stars', dps: ['8.1.5'], cards: [
  { id: 'p8st01', dp: '8.1.5', t: 'con', q: 'What are the two key features of a stellar spectrum, and what does each tell us?',
    a: L(['An approximately <b>black-body continuous spectrum</b> from the hot, dense interior: its peak wavelength and colour give the <b>surface temperature</b> (Wien’s law).', '<b>Absorption lines</b> from cooler gas in the star’s outer atmosphere: they identify the <b>elements, ions and molecules</b> present.']) },
  { id: 'p8st02', dp: '8.1.5', t: 'rec', q: 'List the spectral classes in order, with the colour and temperature range of each.',
    a: P('<b>O B A F G K M</b> (‘Oh Be A Fine Gorilla, Kiss Me’), hottest to coolest. Each class has 10 subclasses (e.g. B0–B9).'), v: 'p8classes' },
  { id: 'p8st03', dp: '8.1.5', t: 'con', q: 'Explain how absorption lines are used to classify stars.',
    a: L(['A star’s temperature decides which particles exist in its atmosphere: higher temperatures <b>break molecules into atoms, then atoms into ions</b>.', 'So each temperature range shows a characteristic pattern of lines: molecules (e.g. TiO) in cool M stars, metals in G and K stars, strong hydrogen in A stars, helium and ionised helium in hot B and O stars.', 'Matching a star’s line pattern (together with its colour) places it in a <b>spectral class</b>, which corresponds to a surface temperature range.']) },
  { id: 'p8st04', dp: '8.1.5', t: 'con', q: 'How is a star’s <b>chemical composition</b> found from its spectrum?',
    a: L(['Cooler gas in the star’s outer layers absorbs wavelengths matching its electron transitions, giving dark lines.', 'Every element has a unique set of lines, so the star’s absorption lines are compared with laboratory spectra of known elements.', 'Matching lines show which elements (and ions or molecules) are present; the relative darkness of lines indicates how much.']) + N('Helium was first identified this way in the Sun, before it was found on Earth.') },
  { id: 'p8st05', dp: '8.1.5', t: 'eq', q: 'How is a star’s <b>translational (radial) velocity</b> found from its spectrum? Give the equation.',
    a: L(['Compare the wavelengths of its absorption lines with laboratory wavelengths of the same element.', 'Lines shifted to longer wavelengths (red shift): moving away. Shorter (blue shift): moving towards us.']) + E('v = c' + F('Δλ', 'λ_0')) + L(['Δλ = observed change in wavelength; λ_0 = wavelength emitted at the source.', 'This gives only the motion <b>along our line of sight</b>. Sideways motion is found by tracking the star’s position against distant objects over long periods.']) },
  { id: 'p8st06', dp: '8.1.5', t: 'calc', q: 'The H-alpha line (656 nm) of Barnard’s star is blue-shifted by 0.24 nm. Estimate its radial speed, and explain why its actual speed (143 km s^{-1}) is larger.',
    a: E('v = c' + F('Δλ', 'λ_0') + ' = 3.00 × 10^8 × ' + F('0.24', '656')) + P('<b>v = 1.1 × 10^5 m s^{-1}</b> (110 km s^{-1}) <b>towards</b> Earth (blue shift).') + P('The Doppler shift only measures the component of velocity along the line of sight. The star also moves sideways (transverse motion), so its total speed is larger.') + N('Your booklet’s answer page says 11 km s^{-1}; the calculation gives 110 km s^{-1}.') },
  { id: 'p8st07', dp: '8.1.5', t: 'con', q: 'How does a spectrum show a star’s <b>rotational velocity</b>?',
    a: L(['One side of a rotating star moves towards us (its light is blue-shifted) while the other side moves away (red-shifted).', 'Each spectral line is a mix of slightly blue- and red-shifted light, so the line is <b>broadened</b>.', 'The faster the rotation, the broader the lines, so the width gives the rotational velocity.', 'In a binary star system, the two stars’ lines alternate between red and blue shift, giving the orbital period and speeds.']) },
  { id: 'p8st08', dp: '8.1.5', t: 'con', q: 'How does a spectrum show the <b>density</b> of a star’s atmosphere? Compare a supergiant with a main sequence star of the same class.',
    a: L(['In a denser atmosphere, atoms collide more often, which slightly disturbs their energy levels and <b>broadens</b> the spectral lines (pressure broadening).', 'A <b>supergiant</b> (e.g. class A3 I) has a huge, very low-density atmosphere: <b>narrow</b> lines.', 'A <b>main sequence</b> star of the same class (A3 V) has a denser atmosphere: <b>broad</b> lines.', 'So for a given temperature, narrower lines mean a more luminous (larger, less dense) star.']) },
  { id: 'p8st09', dp: '8.1.5', t: 'exam', q: 'Outline two mechanisms that could make a star’s spectral line broader than the same line measured in a laboratory. (4 marks)',
    a: L(['<b>Rotation (Doppler broadening)</b>: one edge of the rotating star approaches (blue shift) and the other recedes (red shift), so the line spreads over a range of wavelengths. Faster rotation gives broader lines.', '<b>Pressure (density) broadening</b>: in a dense stellar atmosphere atoms collide more often, disturbing their energy levels, so they absorb a wider range of wavelengths.']) },
  { id: 'p8st10', dp: '8.1.5', t: 'exam', q: 'NESA (3 marks): Diagram 1 is light shone through hydrogen gas; Diagrams 2 and 3 are spectra of the stars Croesus and Dromus. Explain what each spectrum tells us about the star’s motion.',
    vf: 'p8croesus', a: L(['Both stars show the same pattern of lines as hydrogen, so both contain hydrogen.', '<b>Croesus</b>: thin lines, all shifted towards the <b>red</b> end: it is moving <b>away</b> from us and is not rotating noticeably.', '<b>Dromus</b>: much <b>broader</b> lines, so it is <b>rotating</b> (one side approaching, one receding); its lines are shifted slightly towards the <b>violet</b>, so it is moving slightly <b>towards</b> us.']) },
  { id: 'p8st11', dp: '8.1.5', t: 'exam', q: 'NESA 2016 (4 marks): Describe how the spectrum of a star can be used to determine its chemical composition and surface temperature.',
    a: L(['<b>Composition</b>: gases in the star’s cooler outer layers absorb specific wavelengths, producing dark absorption lines. Each element has a unique set of lines, so matching them with laboratory spectra identifies the elements present.', '<b>Temperature (1)</b>: a star approximates a black body. Measure the peak wavelength of its continuous spectrum and use Wien’s law, T = b/λ_{max}.', '<b>Temperature (2)</b>: which lines appear (molecules, neutral atoms or ions) depends on temperature, so the line pattern gives the spectral class and its temperature range.']) }
]});

DECKS.push({ id: 'phys-m8-hr', fa: 'fa1', title: 'The H–R diagram and the life of stars', dps: ['8.1.6'], cards: [
  { id: 'p8hr01', dp: '8.1.6', t: 'def', q: 'What is a <b>Hertzsprung–Russell (H–R) diagram</b>? Describe its axes.',
    a: L(['A graph of stars’ <b>luminosity</b> (or absolute magnitude) against their <b>surface temperature</b> (or colour, or spectral class). Developed independently by Hertzsprung (1911) and Russell (1913).', 'Vertical axis: luminosity compared with the Sun, on a <b>logarithmic</b> scale (or absolute magnitude, where more negative = brighter).', 'Horizontal axis: temperature <b>reversed</b>, hottest (blue, O) on the left to coolest (red, M) on the right, also non-linear.', 'When stars are plotted, they fall into clear groups: main sequence, giants, supergiants and white dwarfs.']), v: 'p8hr' },
  { id: 'p8hr02', dp: '8.1.6', t: 'def', q: 'What is a star’s <b>absolute magnitude</b>, and why is it used on H–R diagrams?',
    a: L(['The apparent brightness a star <b>would have</b> if it were a standard distance of <b>10 parsecs</b> from us.', 'Apparent brightness depends on distance, so using a set distance gives a <b>valid comparison</b> of how much light stars actually emit.', 'The more <b>negative</b> the absolute magnitude, the <b>brighter</b> the star.']) + N('1 parsec ≈ 3.26 light years: the distance at which the Earth’s orbital radius subtends an angle of one second of arc.') },
  { id: 'p8hr03', dp: '8.1.6', t: 'con', q: 'Describe the general trends shown on an H–R diagram.',
    a: L(['<b>Temperature</b> increases from right to left; <b>colour</b> goes from red (right) to blue (left).', '<b>Luminosity</b> increases from bottom to top.', 'Along the main sequence, <b>mass</b> increases towards the upper left, and those stars have <b>shorter lives</b>.', '<b>Diameter</b> increases diagonally towards the upper right: giants and supergiants are huge; white dwarfs (bottom left) are tiny.']) },
  { id: 'p8hr04', dp: '8.1.6', t: 'con', q: 'How can the H–R diagram be used to determine a star’s surface temperature, colour, luminosity and evolutionary stage?',
    a: L(['<b>Surface temperature</b>: read from the horizontal axis (hotter to the left).', '<b>Colour</b>: linked to temperature and spectral class: blue (O, B) on the left through white, yellow, orange to red (M) on the right.', '<b>Luminosity</b>: read from the vertical axis.', '<b>Characteristics and evolutionary stage</b>: the region it falls in. Main sequence = fusing hydrogen in its core; red giant/supergiant = post-main sequence, fusing helium or heavier elements; white dwarf = dead core with no fusion. Position also suggests size and, on the main sequence, mass.']) },
  { id: 'p8hr05', dp: '8.1.6', t: 'con', q: 'Describe <b>main sequence</b> stars and where they lie on the H–R diagram.',
    a: L(['They lie on the diagonal band from top left to bottom right.', 'All fuse <b>hydrogen into helium in their cores</b> (needs a core temperature of at least about 10^7 K).', 'Top left: hot, blue, very luminous (up to about 10^5 × the Sun) and massive (up to about 100 × the Sun).', 'Bottom right: cool, red, dim (about 0.001 × the Sun) and low-mass (red dwarfs).', 'More massive main sequence stars are more luminous.']), v: 'p8ms' },
  { id: 'p8hr06', dp: '8.1.6', t: 'con', q: 'What keeps a main sequence star a stable size, and what happens if this balance is lost?',
    a: L(['<b>Outward</b>: thermal (gas and radiation) pressure from the energy released by fusion in the core.', '<b>Inward</b>: the star’s own <b>gravity</b> pulling its mass towards the centre.', 'On the main sequence these are balanced, so the star stays about the same size.', 'If core fusion slows (hydrogen running low), gravity wins and the core <b>contracts and heats up</b>; once it is hot enough for helium fusion, the extra energy makes the star <b>expand</b> into a red giant.']) },
  { id: 'p8hr07', dp: '8.1.6', t: 'con', q: 'Why do more massive main sequence stars have shorter lifetimes?',
    a: L(['Greater mass means stronger gravity, so a <b>higher core temperature and pressure</b>.', 'Fusion therefore runs much faster: they are far more luminous and use their hydrogen fuel much more quickly.', 'Although they have more fuel, the rate of use rises much faster than the amount of fuel, so they leave the main sequence sooner (about 10^6 years for hot blue stars versus up to about 5 × 10^{10} years for red dwarfs).']) },
  { id: 'p8hr08', dp: '8.1.6', t: 'con', q: 'What happens when a star like the Sun leaves the main sequence? Describe red giants.',
    a: L(['When about 10% of the core hydrogen has fused, core fusion stops; the helium core <b>contracts and heats</b>.', 'Hydrogen keeps fusing in a <b>shell</b> around the core (shell burning), and the outer layers expand and cool: a <b>red giant</b>.', 'Red giants are 10–100 times their main sequence diameter, cool (spectral class K or M) but luminous because they are so large: upper right of the H–R diagram.', 'When the core reaches about 10^8 K, <b>helium fusion</b> (the triple alpha process) begins, making carbon and oxygen.']) },
  { id: 'p8hr09', dp: '8.1.6', t: 'rec', q: 'Describe red supergiants and blue supergiants.',
    a: L(['<b>Red supergiants</b>: very top right of the H–R diagram; the largest stars by volume (radius up to about 1500 × the Sun); cool surfaces (3500–4500 K). Their cores fuse successively heavier elements in shells up to iron. Examples: Betelgeuse, Antares.', '<b>Blue supergiants</b>: top left; spectral class O or B, surface 30 000–50 000 K, about 10–50 solar masses, and much smaller than red supergiants. Very short lives. Example: Rigel.', 'A massive star may swing between blue and red supergiant phases; both end in a <b>supernova</b>.']) },
  { id: 'p8hr10', dp: '8.1.6', t: 'rec', q: 'Describe white dwarfs, neutron stars and black holes.',
    a: L(['<b>White dwarf</b>: bottom left (hot but dim because it is tiny, about Earth-sized). The exposed core of a low- or medium-mass star; no fusion, it just radiates leftover heat. Held up by electron pressure; maximum mass about 1.4 solar masses (the Chandrasekhar limit).', '<b>Neutron star</b>: the collapsed core left by a supernova; radius about 10 km and extremely dense. Fast-spinning neutron stars that we detect as pulses of radiation are <b>pulsars</b>.', '<b>Black hole</b>: if the remnant core is more than about 2–3 solar masses, nothing stops its collapse; within the event horizon not even light can escape.']) },
  { id: 'p8hr11', dp: '8.1.6', t: 'con', q: 'Outline how a star’s evolutionary path depends on its initial mass.',
    a: P('The <b>initial mass</b> decides where a star starts on the main sequence, how long it stays there and how it ends.'), v: 'p8evol' },
  { id: 'p8hr12', dp: '8.1.6', t: 'exam', q: 'Describe the life cycle of a star of about one solar mass, referring to its path on the H–R diagram.',
    a: OL(['A cloud of gas and dust contracts under gravity into a <b>protostar</b>, heating up.', 'When the core is hot enough for hydrogen fusion it joins the <b>main sequence</b> (middle of the band) and stays there for billions of years.', 'When core hydrogen runs low, the core contracts and heats while the outer layers expand and cool: it moves <b>up and to the right</b> to become a <b>red giant</b>, where helium fusion begins.', 'It sheds its outer layers, and the hot exposed core moves <b>down and to the left</b> to become a <b>white dwarf</b>, which slowly cools (a black dwarf eventually).']) + N('‘Moving’ on the H–R diagram means changing stage of life, not moving through space.') },
  { id: 'p8hr13', dp: '8.1.6', t: 'con', q: 'Why are black dwarfs only a prediction? Correct a common mistake about brown dwarfs.',
    a: L(['A white dwarf has no fusion, so it must slowly cool until it no longer glows: a <b>black dwarf</b>.', 'Cooling takes far longer than the current age of the Universe, so <b>no black dwarfs exist yet</b>.', '<b>Brown dwarfs</b> are not cooled white dwarfs: they are ‘failed stars’ that never had enough mass to start hydrogen fusion.']) + N('Your booklet says white dwarfs become brown dwarfs and then black dwarfs, and its answer page cites brown dwarfs as evidence of cooling. A white dwarf cools directly into a black dwarf.') },
  { id: 'p8hr14', dp: '8.1.6', t: 'rec', q: 'Describe red dwarfs.',
    a: L(['Low-mass main sequence stars (less than about 40% of the Sun’s mass) at the bottom right of the H–R diagram.', 'Cool cores, so they fuse hydrogen slowly and are very dim.', 'Convection mixes their hydrogen, so they use more of it; their lifetimes are longer than the current age of the Universe.', 'The most common type of star (e.g. Proxima Centauri), but none are visible to the naked eye.']) },
  { id: 'p8hr15', dp: '8.1.6', t: 'exam', q: 'True or false? (a) Hotter stars burn their fuel less quickly than cooler stars. (b) White dwarfs produce energy by fusing their last hydrogen. (c) Large hot stars are more likely to supernova than smaller, cooler stars. (d) There are more black dwarfs than white dwarfs.',
    a: L(['(a) <b>False</b>: hotter (more massive) stars fuse their fuel much faster.', '(b) <b>False</b>: white dwarfs have no fusion; they only radiate stored heat.', '(c) <b>True</b>: only massive stars end in supernovae.', '(d) <b>False</b>: no black dwarfs exist yet.']) + N('Your booklet’s answer page (Worksheet 26, Q5) marks (b) true and (c) false; its own notes (no fusion in white dwarfs; supernovae only for massive stars) support the answers here.') }
]});

DECKS.push({ id: 'phys-m8-ns', fa: 'fa1', title: 'E = mc² and nucleosynthesis in stars', dps: ['8.1.3', '8.1.7'], cards: [
  { id: 'p8ns01', dp: '8.1.3', t: 'con', q: 'How does Einstein’s mass–energy equivalence explain the energy released by fusion in stars?',
    a: L(['In each fusion reaction the total mass of the <b>products is less than the total mass of the reactants</b>: m_{products} &lt; m_{reactants}.', 'This lost mass (the <b>mass defect</b>) is converted to energy: E = Δmc^2.', 'Because c^2 is huge, a tiny mass loss releases an enormous amount of energy, mostly as kinetic energy of the products and gamma photons.']) + N('Be specific in exams: state that the products have less mass than the reactants. Just writing ‘mass defect’ is not enough.') },
  { id: 'p8ns02', dp: '8.1.7', t: 'eq', q: 'Write the three steps of the <b>proton–proton (PP I) chain</b>, with the energy released by each.',
    a: P('Discovered by Hans Bethe (1938). It is the main fusion process in the Sun.'), v: 'p8ppd' },
  { id: 'p8ns03', dp: '8.1.7', t: 'eq', q: 'Write the overall equation for the proton–proton chain and the total energy released.',
    a: E(`4 ${PR} → ${AL} + 2 ${BP} + 2ν + 2γ`) + P('Total energy ≈ <b>26.7 MeV</b> per helium nucleus: 2(1.44 + 5.49) + 12.86 = 26.72 MeV.') + N('You need to remember this summary equation.') },
  { id: 'p8ns04', dp: '8.1.7', t: 'calc', q: 'The Sun outputs 4 × 10^{26} W. Using 26.72 MeV per PP chain, find the number of chains per second and the mass the Sun loses each second.',
    a: OL(['Energy per chain = 26.72 × 10^6 × 1.602 × 10^{-19} = 4.28 × 10^{-12} J', 'Chains per second = ' + F('4 × 10^{26}', '4.28 × 10^{-12}') + ' = <b>9.3 × 10^{37} s^{-1}</b>', 'Mass lost per second: m = ' + F('E', 'c^2') + ' = ' + F('4 × 10^{26}', '(3.00 × 10^8)^2') + ' = <b>4.4 × 10^9 kg</b> (about 4 million tonnes every second)']) + N('Over 5 billion years this is about 7 × 10^{26} kg, only about 0.035% of the Sun’s 2 × 10^{30} kg.') },
  { id: 'p8ns05', dp: '8.1.7', t: 'eq', q: 'Describe the <b>CNO cycle</b> and give its overall equation.',
    a: E(`4 ${PR} → ${AL} + 2 ${BP} + 2ν + 3γ`) + L(['Protons are added one at a time to carbon-12, building nitrogen and oxygen isotopes (two of which undergo β⁺ decay), until nitrogen-15 plus a proton produces <b>carbon-12 and a helium nucleus</b>.', 'Carbon, nitrogen and oxygen are not used up: they act as <b>catalysts</b>.', 'Net result: four protons fuse into one helium nucleus, releasing about 26.7 MeV (the same as the PP chain).']), v: 'p8cno' },
  { id: 'p8ns06', dp: '8.1.7', t: 'con', q: 'Which stars use the proton–proton chain and which use the CNO cycle? Why?',
    a: L(['<b>Lower-mass stars</b> (less than about 1.5 solar masses) with core temperatures of about 10–15 million K, like the Sun: mainly the <b>PP chain</b>. They are on the lower right of the main sequence.', '<b>Higher-mass stars</b> with core temperatures above about 15 million K: mainly the <b>CNO cycle</b>. They are on the upper left of the main sequence.', 'The CNO cycle needs higher temperatures because protons must overcome the larger electrostatic repulsion of C, N and O nuclei (6–8 protons). Its rate rises steeply with temperature.', 'The CNO cycle also needs some C, N and O present, from earlier generations of stars. It supplies less than 2% of the Sun’s energy.']) },
  { id: 'p8ns07', dp: '8.1.7', t: 'eq', q: 'Describe the <b>triple alpha process</b>. In which stars does it occur?',
    a: L([`Two helium nuclei fuse to form unstable beryllium-8: ${AL} + ${AL} ⇌ ${NU(8, 4, 'Be')}`, `Before it decays, a third helium nucleus fuses with it: ${NU(8, 4, 'Be')} + ${AL} → ${NU(12, 6, 'C')} + γ`, `Carbon can capture another helium nucleus: ${NU(12, 6, 'C')} + ${AL} → ${NU(16, 8, 'O')} + γ`, 'Needs a core temperature of about <b>10^8 K</b>, so it happens in <b>post-main sequence</b> stars: red giants and supergiants.', 'It is the main source of the carbon and oxygen in the Universe, including in our bodies. In stars below about 2–3 solar masses it starts suddenly: the <b>helium flash</b>.']) },
  { id: 'p8ns08', dp: '8.1.7', t: 'con', q: 'How do massive stars make elements up to iron, and why does fusion stop at iron?',
    a: L(['In red supergiants, the core contracts and heats in stages, so successively heavier elements fuse in <b>shells</b>: H → He → C → Ne, O → Si (and Mg) → iron (and nickel).', 'Iron sits at the <b>peak of the binding energy per nucleon curve</b>. Fusing iron into heavier nuclei would absorb energy instead of releasing it.', 'So an inert <b>iron core</b> builds up with no energy output to resist gravity.']) },
  { id: 'p8ns09', dp: '8.1.7', t: 'con', q: 'How do supernovae produce and spread elements heavier than iron?',
    a: L(['When the iron core reaches about 1.4 solar masses it collapses: protons and electrons are forced together into neutrons (and neutrinos).', 'The outer layers fall in, rebound off the core and are blown apart in a <b>supernova</b>.', 'Huge numbers of neutrons are captured by nuclei, building elements <b>heavier than iron</b> (e.g. gold, silver, uranium).', 'The material is scattered into the interstellar medium, from which later stars and planets (like the Sun and Earth) form. The remaining core becomes a neutron star or black hole.']) },
  { id: 'p8ns10', dp: '8.1.7', t: 'exam', q: 'NESA (4 marks): Describe TWO processes which account for energy production in stars.',
    a: L(['<b>Proton–proton chain</b>: the main process in lower-mass stars like the Sun (core about 10–15 million K). Protons fuse in steps to deuterium, then helium-3, and two helium-3 nuclei fuse to helium-4, releasing two protons. Overall four hydrogen nuclei form one helium nucleus, two positrons, two neutrinos and gamma rays, releasing about 26.7 MeV.', '<b>CNO cycle</b>: dominant in hotter, more massive stars (core above about 15 million K). Carbon-12 captures protons in a cycle through nitrogen and oxygen isotopes; carbon, nitrogen and oxygen act as catalysts and carbon-12 is regenerated, with the same overall result: four protons → one helium nucleus.', 'In both, the products have less mass than the reactants, and the lost mass becomes energy, E = Δmc^2.']) + N('Post-main sequence stars also release energy by the triple alpha process (helium → carbon).') },
  { id: 'p8ns11', dp: '8.1.3', t: 'exam', q: 'NESA (7 marks): “One of the most important equations in all of physics is Einstein’s E = mc^2.” Justify this statement. List the ideas to include.',
    a: L(['<b>Stars</b>: fusion (PP chain, CNO cycle) releases energy because the products have less mass than the reactants.', '<b>Nuclear fission</b>: reactors and bombs, where mass lost when heavy nuclei split becomes energy.', '<b>Binding energy and mass defect</b>: explains why a nucleus has less mass than its separate nucleons, and the binding energy curve.', '<b>Radioactive decay</b>: the energy carried away by α, β and γ radiation comes from the mass difference.', '<b>Big Bang and particle accelerators</b>: energy converts into matter (pair production), and high-energy collisions create new particles.', '<b>Judgement</b>: few single equations apply across so many fields, so it is justified as one of the most important in physics.']) },
  { id: 'p8ns12', dp: '8.1.7', t: 'con', q: 'How do neutrinos provide evidence that the Sun is powered by fusion?',
    a: L(['The PP chain produces neutrinos, which interact so weakly with matter that they leave the Sun’s core within seconds (light takes millions of years to get out).', 'Neutrinos from the Sun are detected on Earth, even at night, having passed straight through the Earth.', 'The number detected agrees with the number predicted by fusion models, further supporting the theory of fusion in the Sun.']) }
]});

/* ================= Inquiry Question 8.2: Structure of the Atom ================= */
DECKS.push({ id: 'phys-m8-cr', fa: 'fa2', title: 'Cathode rays and charges in fields', dps: ['8.2.1'], cards: [
  { id: 'p8cr01', dp: '8.2.1', t: 'def', q: 'Give the NESA definitions of a <b>field</b>, <b>charge</b> and <b>electric field</b>. Which way does an electric field point?',
    a: L(['<b>Field</b>: a region in which a body experiences a force due to the effects of another body (its mass, charge or magnetic properties).', '<b>Charge</b>: the intrinsic electrical nature of a body; may be positive or negative.', '<b>Electric field</b>: a region in which a stationary electric charge experiences a force due to the influence of another charged object.', 'Direction: the direction of the force on a <b>positive</b> test charge, so away from positive charges and towards negative ones.']) },
  { id: 'p8cr02', dp: '8.2.1', t: 'eq', q: 'Give the equations for a charge in a uniform electric field between parallel plates.',
    a: E('E = ' + F('V', 'd') + '    F = qE    W = qV = qEd') + L(['E = field strength (V m^{-1} or N C^{-1}); V = potential difference (V); d = plate separation (m)', 'q = charge (C), always converted to SI (e.g. 2.0 mC = 2.0 × 10^{-3} C)', 'Charge on an electron: −1.602 × 10^{-19} C; a proton has the same size charge but positive (not on the data sheet: remember it).']) },
  { id: 'p8cr03', dp: '8.2.1', t: 'con', q: 'A charge q starts from rest and is accelerated across a potential difference V. Write the energy relationship and its conditions.',
    a: E('W = qV = ΔK = ½mv^2') + L(['The work done by the field becomes kinetic energy. Not on your data sheet.', 'Only valid if the particle <b>starts from rest</b> and moves through the <b>whole</b> potential difference.', 'Doubling the plate spacing at the same V halves E and the force, but the particle accelerates over twice the distance, so it reaches the <b>same speed</b>.', 'A particle fired in midway between the plates and striking one plate only moves through <b>half</b> the potential difference (HSC 2020 Q34).']) + N('1 eV is the energy gained by an electron across 1 V, so 10.0 keV electrons have been accelerated through 10 kV.') },
  { id: 'p8cr04', dp: '8.2.1', t: 'eq', q: 'What force acts on a charge moving through a magnetic field, and how do you find its direction?',
    a: E('F = qv_{⊥}B = qvB sin θ') + L(['θ = angle between the velocity and the field: maximum force at 90°, <b>zero</b> when moving parallel to the field; a stationary charge feels no force.', '<b>Right-hand (slap) rule</b> for a positive charge: thumb along v, fingers along B, force out of the palm.', 'For a <b>negative</b> charge, find the force as if positive, then reverse it.']) },
  { id: 'p8cr05', dp: '8.2.1', t: 'eq', q: 'Why does a charged particle move in a circle in a uniform magnetic field? Derive the radius.',
    a: L(['The magnetic force is always perpendicular to the velocity, so it changes the direction but not the speed: <b>uniform circular motion</b>.', 'The magnetic force supplies the centripetal force:']) + E('qvB = ' + F('mv^2', 'r') + '  →  r = ' + F('mv', 'qB')) + P('If the particle leaves the field, it continues in a straight line.') },
  { id: 'p8cr06', dp: '8.2.1', t: 'con', q: 'Describe what was observed as more air was pumped out of a discharge tube, and explain it.',
    a: L(['At low pressure (Geissler tubes, about 10^{-3} atm) a <b>glow</b> fills the tube instead of a spark.', 'At lower pressure (Crookes tubes, below 10^{-6} atm) a dark space spreads from the cathode until the tube is dark, but the <b>glass at the anode end glows</b>.', '<b>Explanation</b>: with fewer gas atoms, electrons from the cathode travel further before colliding. Eventually they cross the whole tube at high speed, fly past the anode and hit the glass, exciting its atoms so it fluoresces.', 'Early researchers thought the rays came from the cathode, so they called them <b>cathode rays</b>.']) + N('In Physics the cathode is the electrode connected to the <b>negative</b> terminal; the anode is positive.') },
  { id: 'p8cr07', dp: '8.2.1', t: 'prac', q: 'What did the <b>Maltese cross tube</b> show about cathode rays?',
    a: L(['A metal Maltese cross was placed in the path of the rays; a <b>sharp shadow</b> of the cross appeared in the glow at the end of the tube.', 'Cathode rays travel in <b>straight lines</b>.', 'They cannot pass through a reasonably thick piece of metal.', 'They travel <b>from the cathode towards the anode</b>.']) },
  { id: 'p8cr08', dp: '8.2.1', t: 'prac', q: 'What did the <b>paddle wheel tube</b> show, and why didn’t it settle whether cathode rays were particles or waves?',
    a: L(['A light paddle wheel placed in the beam <b>turned</b> and rolled away from the cathode.', 'This suggested the rays had <b>momentum</b> (p = mv), so could be particles with mass, travelling from cathode to anode.', 'However, a Crookes radiometer was thought to be spun by the energy of light waves, so the result could not decide between waves and particles.']) },
  { id: 'p8cr09', dp: '8.2.1', t: 'prac', q: 'What did experiments with a <b>fluorescent screen and a magnet</b> show about cathode rays?',
    a: L(['A slit made a flat beam, and a fluorescent screen showed its path (the rays themselves are invisible).', 'A magnet <b>deflected</b> the beam, in the direction expected for <b>negatively charged particles</b> (Crookes).', 'The beam also <b>spread out</b> along the tube, suggesting the particles repel each other, so they must be charged.']) },
  { id: 'p8cr10', dp: '8.2.1', t: 'prac', q: 'What did Hertz (1883), Schuster (1890) and Perrin find about cathode rays and electric charge?',
    a: L(['<b>Hertz</b>: saw no deflection between charged plates and concluded cathode rays were <b>waves</b>. The deflection was real but too small for his apparatus to show.', '<b>Schuster</b>: showed the rays were repelled by a negative plate and attracted to a positive plate: <b>negatively charged particles</b>.', '<b>Perrin</b>: collected the rays in a closed metal cylinder connected to an electroscope, which became <b>negatively charged</b>.']) },
  { id: 'p8cr11', dp: '8.2.1', t: 'exam', q: 'Explain why the apparently inconsistent behaviour of cathode rays caused debate about whether they were charged particles or electromagnetic waves.',
    a: L(['<b>Particle-like evidence</b>: deflected by magnetic fields like negative charges; turned a paddle wheel (momentum); charged an electroscope negatively.', '<b>Wave-like evidence</b>: travelled in straight lines and cast sharp shadows (like light); Hertz found no deflection by an electric field, and EM waves are not deflected by electric fields.', 'Neither model explained all the results, and Hertz’s reputation added weight to the wave view.', 'The debate was settled by Thomson (1897): with a better vacuum, cathode rays <b>were</b> deflected by electric fields, and their speed was far below the speed of light, so they are <b>negatively charged particles</b> (electrons).']) },
  { id: 'p8cr12', dp: '8.2.1', t: 'rec', q: 'List the properties of cathode rays.',
    a: L(['Produced at the cathode (negative electrode) in an evacuated tube and accelerated towards the anode.', 'The same whatever metal the cathode is made from.', 'Travel in straight lines and cast sharp shadows.', 'Have energy and momentum, so can do work (e.g. turn a paddle wheel).', 'Deflected by electric and magnetic fields as <b>negative</b> charges.', 'Make glass and some materials fluoresce; produce some chemical changes (e.g. darken silver salts).', 'They are streams of <b>electrons</b>.']) }
]});

DECKS.push({ id: 'phys-m8-tm', fa: 'fa2', title: 'Thomson and Millikan: properties of the electron', dps: ['8.2.1'], cards: [
  { id: 'p8tm01', dp: '8.2.1', t: 'con', q: 'Why did Thomson (1897) measure the charge-to-mass ratio of cathode rays, and what apparatus did he use?',
    a: L(['No method existed to measure the charge or the mass of cathode rays separately, so he measured the <b>ratio q/m</b> and compared it with known particles (e.g. the hydrogen ion).', 'A highly evacuated tube: cathode and anode with slits to make a narrow beam.', '<b>Parallel plates</b> with a potential difference: a uniform electric field.', '<b>Current-carrying coils</b> outside the tube: a uniform magnetic field at right angles to the electric field.', 'A fluorescent screen with a scale to show where the beam lands.']) },
  { id: 'p8tm02', dp: '8.2.1', t: 'con', q: 'Explain Thomson’s first procedure: how did he find the speed of the cathode rays?',
    a: L(['He deflected the beam with the electric field, then adjusted the magnetic field until the beam went through <b>undeflected</b> (the ‘null method’).', 'Then the electric and magnetic forces are equal and opposite:']) + E('qE = qvB  →  v = ' + F('E', 'B') + ' = ' + F('V', 'Bd')) + P('E (from V/d) and B are both measurable, so v can be calculated.'), v: 'p8crossed' },
  { id: 'p8tm03', dp: '8.2.1', t: 'con', q: 'Explain Thomson’s second procedure and derive his expression for q/m.',
    a: L(['He switched off the electric field, so the magnetic field alone bent the beam into a <b>circular arc</b>, and measured its radius r.', 'The magnetic force provides the centripetal force:']) + E('qvB = ' + F('mv^2', 'r') + '  →  ' + F('q', 'm') + ' = ' + F('v', 'Br')) + P('Substituting v = E/B from the first procedure:') + E(F('q', 'm') + ' = ' + F('E', 'B^2r')) + N('These two equations are not on your data sheet.') },
  { id: 'p8tm04', dp: '8.2.1', t: 'rec', q: 'What were Thomson’s results and conclusions?',
    a: L(['Speed about 1 × 10^7 m s^{-1}: far below the speed of light, so cathode rays are <b>not EM waves</b>.', 'q/m ≈ <b>1.76 × 10^{11} C kg^{-1}</b>.', 'q/m was the same for any accelerating voltage and any cathode metal, so these particles are a <b>fundamental part of all atoms</b>.', 'q/m was about 1800 times that of the hydrogen ion, so (assuming similar charge) the particles are far less massive than the smallest atom: <b>the atom is divisible</b>.', 'He concluded cathode rays are extremely light, fast, negatively charged particles, later called <b>electrons</b>. This disproved Dalton’s indivisible atom.']) },
  { id: 'p8tm05', dp: '8.2.1', t: 'calc', q: 'Cathode rays pass undeflected between plates 5.0 cm apart with 500 V across them, in a 10 mT magnetic field. Calculate their speed.',
    a: OL(['E = ' + F('V', 'd') + ' = ' + F('500', '0.050') + ' = 1.0 × 10^4 V m^{-1}', 'Undeflected: qE = qvB, so v = ' + F('E', 'B') + ' = ' + F('1.0 × 10^4', '10 × 10^{-3}'), '<b>v = 1.0 × 10^6 m s^{-1}</b>']) + N('The magnetic field must be perpendicular to both the beam and the electric field. Increase B from zero until it exactly cancels the electric deflection.') },
  { id: 'p8tm06', dp: '8.2.1', t: 'calc', q: 'Electrons from a hot filament are accelerated through 300 V, then bent into a circle of radius 10 cm by a 5.0 × 10^{-4} T field. Find their speed and q/m.',
    a: OL(['qV = ½mv^2, so v = √(2qV/m) = √(2 × 1.602 × 10^{-19} × 300 ÷ 9.109 × 10^{-31}) = <b>1.0 × 10^7 m s^{-1}</b> (1.027 × 10^7)', 'qvB = mv^2/r, so ' + F('q', 'm') + ' = ' + F('v', 'Br') + ' = ' + F('1.027 × 10^7', '5.0 × 10^{-4} × 0.10'), '<b>q/m = 2.1 × 10^{11} C kg^{-1}</b>']) + N('Carry the unrounded speed into the second step.') },
  { id: 'p8tm07', dp: '8.2.1', t: 'exam', q: 'NESA 2017 sample (3 marks): Particles accelerated from rest through 100–500 V give a graph of v^2 against V with gradient about 1.1 × 10^{10} m^2 s^{-2} V^{-1}. A student says they are electrons. Justify whether this is correct.',
    a: OL(['qV = ½mv^2, so ' + F('v^2', 'V') + ' = ' + F('2q', 'm') + ': the gradient equals 2q/m.', 'q/m = gradient ÷ 2 ≈ <b>5.4 × 10^9 C kg^{-1}</b>', 'For electrons, q/m = ' + F('1.602 × 10^{-19}', '9.109 × 10^{-31}') + ' = 1.76 × 10^{11} C kg^{-1}, about 30 times larger.', 'So the hypothesis is <b>incorrect</b>: the particles are not electrons.']) },
  { id: 'p8tm08', dp: '8.2.1', t: 'prac', q: 'Describe Millikan’s oil drop apparatus (1909).',
    a: L(['An <b>atomiser</b> sprays a fine mist of oil drops into the space above two horizontal parallel plates.', 'Some drops fall through a hole into the region between the plates.', '<b>X-rays</b> ionise the air so drops pick up charge.', 'A variable voltage across the plates gives a uniform electric field that pushes up on charged drops.', 'Drops are viewed through a <b>microscope</b>, and the voltage adjusted until a drop is held stationary (or moves at constant velocity).']) },
  { id: 'p8tm09', dp: '8.2.1', t: 'eq', q: 'Derive the charge on a stationary oil drop. How did Millikan find the drop’s mass?',
    a: P('For a stationary drop the forces balance (Newton’s second law, ΣF = 0): the electric force up equals the weight down.') + E('qE = mg  and  E = ' + F('V', 'd') + '  →  q = ' + F('mgd', 'V')) + L(['q is the <b>total</b> charge on the drop, not the charge of one electron.', '<b>Mass</b>: with the field off, he measured the drop’s terminal velocity as it fell, used fluid mechanics to find its radius, then used the known density of the oil.']) + N('Not on your data sheet.') },
  { id: 'p8tm10', dp: '8.2.1', t: 'con', q: 'What did Millikan’s results show?',
    a: L(['The charge on every drop was always a <b>whole-number multiple</b> of a smallest value, never anything in between.', 'Charge is <b>quantised</b>: it comes in ‘packets’.', 'The smallest unit, the <b>elementary charge</b> e = 1.6 × 10^{-19} C, is the charge of one electron.', 'Combined with Thomson’s q/m, this gave the electron’s mass (about 9.1 × 10^{-31} kg). Nobel Prize 1923.']) + N('One sentence in your booklet says ‘Thomson found it was always an integer multiple’; it was Millikan.') },
  { id: 'p8tm11', dp: '8.2.1', t: 'calc', q: 'A 2.5 × 10^{-15} kg oil drop is held stationary between plates 3.0 mm apart (top plate positive) at 75, 105, 175, 260 and 550 V. Find the charge for each voltage, with polarity.',
    a: P('q = ' + F('mgd', 'V') + ' = ' + F('2.5 × 10^{-15} × 9.8 × 3.0 × 10^{-3}', 'V') + ' = ' + F('7.35 × 10^{-17}', 'V')) + L(['75 V: 9.8 × 10^{-19} C', '105 V: 7.0 × 10^{-19} C', '175 V: 4.2 × 10^{-19} C', '260 V: 2.8 × 10^{-19} C', '550 V: 1.3 × 10^{-19} C']) + P('All <b>negative</b>: the electric force must be upwards, towards the positive top plate.') + N('Your booklet’s answers give 1.40 and 2.81 × 10^{-19} C for the last two; 550 V gives 1.34 × 10^{-19} C.') },
  { id: 'p8tm12', dp: '8.2.1', t: 'exam', q: 'Using the oil drop charges 9.8, 7.0, 4.2, 2.8 and 1.3 (× 10^{-19} C), assess the hypothesis that the smallest charge is one electronic charge.',
    a: L(['The charges are roughly in the ratio 7 : 5 : 3 : 2 : 1, so they are close to whole-number multiples of a base value of about 1.4 × 10^{-19} C: <b>consistent with quantised charge</b>.', 'But the accepted value of e is 1.60 × 10^{-19} C. The smallest charge is about 15% too small, and the others are not whole-number multiples of 1.60 × 10^{-19} C (e.g. 9.8 ÷ 1.6 = 6.1, 7.0 ÷ 1.6 = 4.4).', 'Allowing for uncertainty, this suggests a <b>systematic error</b> (e.g. in the drop’s mass or the plate spacing).', '<b>Judgement</b>: the hypothesis is only partly supported; quantisation is shown, but the base value does not match e.']) },
  { id: 'p8tm13', dp: '8.2.1', t: 'exam', q: '2021 HSC Q23 (4 marks): Describe how Millikan and Thomson each used fields to determine properties of the electron.',
    a: L(['<b>Thomson</b> used perpendicular <b>electric and magnetic fields</b>. Balancing the forces (qE = qvB) gave the speed of the electrons; the magnetic field alone bent them into a circle, giving the <b>charge-to-mass ratio</b>.', '<b>Millikan</b> used an <b>electric field</b> to balance the <b>gravitational</b> force on charged oil drops (qE = mg). This gave the charge on each drop, showing charge is quantised and giving the <b>charge of an electron</b>.']) + N('‘Describe’ means give characteristics and features; you don’t need every step of the derivations.') },
  { id: 'p8tm14', dp: '8.2.1', t: 'exam', q: 'NESA 2019 sample (6 marks): Different numbers of dominoes are sealed in seven boxes and weighed; the smallest difference in mass between boxes is 4.3 g and all other differences are multiples of it. Assess this as a model of Millikan’s experiment.',
    a: L(['<b>Analysis</b>: every difference is a whole-number multiple of 4.3 g, so the mass is ‘quantised’; one domino is probably 4.3 g (more boxes would make this more certain).', '<b>Comparison</b>: Millikan found every drop’s charge was a whole-number multiple of 1.6 × 10^{-19} C and inferred that this was the charge of one electron.', '<b>Limitations</b>: masses, not charges; no fields or forces are balanced; you cannot open the boxes, just as Millikan could not see how many electrons were on a drop.', '<b>Judgement</b>: very effective for modelling Millikan’s method of <b>analysis</b> (finding a fundamental unit from multiples), though not his apparatus.']) }
]});

DECKS.push({ id: 'phys-m8-nu', fa: 'fa2', title: 'The nuclear atom: Rutherford and Chadwick', dps: ['8.2.2'], cards: [
  { id: 'p8nu01', dp: '8.2.2', t: 'rec', q: 'Summarise the changing models of the atom from Dalton to Bohr.',
    a: P('Each model changed when experimental evidence appeared that the current model could not explain.'), v: 'p8models' },
  { id: 'p8nu02', dp: '8.2.2', t: 'con', q: 'Describe Thomson’s ‘plum pudding’ model and why he proposed it.',
    a: L(['Thomson had discovered the negatively charged electron, but atoms are neutral, so there must also be positive charge.', '<b>Model</b>: a sphere of evenly spread <b>positive</b> charge, with the negative electrons scattered through it like plums in a pudding.', 'It replaced Dalton’s indivisible ‘billiard ball’ atom.']) },
  { id: 'p8nu03', dp: '8.2.2', t: 'prac', q: 'Describe the Geiger–Marsden experiment and what Rutherford expected to happen.',
    a: L(['<b>Alpha particles</b> (small, fast, positively charged) from a radioactive source were fired at a very <b>thin gold foil</b> in a vacuum.', 'A movable <b>zinc sulfide scintillation screen</b> (viewed through a microscope) gave a flash where each alpha particle landed, so deflection angles could be counted.', '<b>Expected</b> (plum pudding): positive charge spread evenly gives only weak forces, so the alpha particles should pass through with only <b>small deflections</b> of a few degrees.']) },
  { id: 'p8nu04', dp: '8.2.2', t: 'con', q: 'What were the results of the Geiger–Marsden experiment?',
    a: L(['<b>Most</b> alpha particles passed straight through, undeflected.', '<b>Some</b> were deflected through small angles.', 'A <b>very small number</b> (about 1 in 8000) were deflected by <b>more than 90°</b>; some bounced almost straight back.']) + N('Rutherford: “It was almost as incredible as if you had fired a fifteen-inch shell at a piece of tissue paper and it came back and hit you.”') },
  { id: 'p8nu05', dp: '8.2.2', t: 'con', q: 'How did Rutherford interpret the Geiger–Marsden results? State his model of the atom (1911).',
    a: L(['Most pass straight through, so the atom is <b>mostly empty space</b>.', 'A few are deflected through large angles by strong electrostatic repulsion, so all the positive charge and nearly all the mass must be concentrated in a tiny, dense <b>nucleus</b>.', 'Model: a small, dense, <b>positive nucleus</b> containing most of the mass; <b>electrons orbit</b> it in circular paths (like planets around the Sun), held by electrostatic attraction.']) + N('Rutherford calculated how many alpha particles should scatter through each angle; Geiger’s later measurements matched these predictions.') },
  { id: 'p8nu06', dp: '8.2.2', t: 'con', q: 'Why could the plum pudding model not explain the large-angle scattering?',
    a: L(['In the plum pudding atom the positive charge is spread through the whole atom, so an alpha particle feels roughly equal forces from all around: the net force is small.', 'The electrons are far too light and their charges too small to deflect a heavy, fast alpha particle much.', 'So no arrangement could turn an alpha particle back. Only a concentrated positive charge (a nucleus) produces a strong enough repulsion.']) },
  { id: 'p8nu07', dp: '8.2.2', t: 'con', q: 'How big is the nucleus compared with the atom?',
    a: L(['Hydrogen atom radius ≈ 2.1 × 10^{-11} m; proton radius ≈ 0.85 × 10^{-15} m (0.85 fm).', 'The atom is about <b>2.5 × 10^4</b> times wider than its nucleus.', 'If an atom were 10 m across, its nucleus would be only about 0.4 mm across, yet it holds about 99.9% of the atom’s mass.']) },
  { id: 'p8nu08', dp: '8.2.2', t: 'con', q: 'What were <b>canal rays</b> (Goldstein, 1886), and what did they show?',
    a: L(['In a low-pressure tube with a <b>perforated cathode</b>, rays travelled through the holes in the opposite direction to cathode rays.', 'They were <b>positively charged gas ions</b>, formed when cathode rays knocked electrons off gas atoms.', 'Unlike cathode rays, their <b>mass and q/m depended on the gas</b>; some carried multiples of the basic unit of charge; they were deflected the opposite way to cathode rays.', 'The <b>largest q/m was for hydrogen</b>: early evidence of the positive particle we now call the proton.']) },
  { id: 'p8nu09', dp: '8.2.2', t: 'eq', q: 'How did Rutherford identify the proton (1917–1919)? Write the nuclear equation.',
    a: L(['He fired high-energy alpha particles at <b>nitrogen</b>, producing oxygen-17 and hydrogen nuclei: the first artificial <b>transmutation</b>.', 'Every transmutation of this kind released hydrogen nuclei, and every nuclear charge was a whole number of hydrogen-nucleus charges, so the hydrogen nucleus is a <b>fundamental part of all nuclei</b>.', 'He named it the <b>proton</b> (in print by 1920).']) + E(`${AL} + ${NU(14, 7, 'N')} → ${NU(17, 8, 'O')} + ${PR}`) },
  { id: 'p8nu10', dp: '8.2.2', t: 'con', q: 'Why did scientists suspect a neutral particle in the nucleus before Chadwick’s discovery?',
    a: L(['Most of an atom’s mass is in the nucleus, but the protons alone could not account for it: helium has atomic mass 4 but only 2 protons.', 'Electrons are far too light to make up the difference.', 'Rutherford proposed an uncharged particle of about proton mass (he pictured a proton–electron pair), which he called the <b>neutron</b>.']) },
  { id: 'p8nu11', dp: '8.2.2', t: 'con', q: 'What did Bothe and Becker, and then the Joliot-Curies, observe before Chadwick’s experiment?',
    a: L(['<b>Bothe and Becker</b>: alpha particles fired at <b>beryllium</b> produced a <b>neutral</b> radiation (not deflected by electric or magnetic fields) that could penetrate 200 mm of lead. They thought it was high-energy gamma rays.', '<b>Joliot-Curies</b>: when this radiation hit <b>paraffin wax</b> (rich in hydrogen), it knocked out <b>protons</b> with about 5 MeV of energy. They also assumed gamma rays.']) },
  { id: 'p8nu12', dp: '8.2.2', t: 'con', q: 'Why couldn’t the ‘beryllium radiation’ be gamma rays?',
    a: L(['Applying conservation of energy and momentum, a gamma photon would need about <b>50 MeV</b> to give a proton 5 MeV.', 'But the alpha particles that produced the radiation had only about 5 MeV: a tenfold increase in energy is impossible.', 'Far more protons were ejected than gamma rays could produce, since highly penetrating gamma rays interact rarely.', 'A <b>neutral particle with about the same mass as a proton</b> could transfer 5 MeV to a proton easily, like one billiard ball hitting another.']) },
  { id: 'p8nu13', dp: '8.2.2', t: 'exam', q: '2010 HSC (3 marks): James Chadwick discovered the neutron in 1932. How did Chadwick apply conservation laws?',
    a: L(['Chadwick bombarded <b>beryllium with alpha particles</b>, producing an unknown neutral radiation, which he directed at <b>paraffin wax</b>, knocking out protons.', 'He measured the speed (and so the kinetic energy and momentum) of the ejected protons, and of recoiling nuclei from other targets such as nitrogen.', 'Applying <b>conservation of momentum and conservation of energy</b> to these collisions, he calculated the unknown particle’s mass: about the same as a proton.', 'Because it was uncharged, he concluded it was a new neutral particle, the <b>neutron</b>, not gamma radiation.']) + N('Markers noted many students used the wrong bombarding particle, or did not name the conservation laws.') },
  { id: 'p8nu14', dp: '8.2.2', t: 'eq', q: 'Write the nuclear equation for the reaction that produced Chadwick’s neutrons. How did the neutron explain isotopes?',
    a: E(`${AL} + ${NU(9, 4, 'Be')} → ${NU(12, 6, 'C')} + ${NE}`) + L(['Neutron mass: 1.675 × 10^{-27} kg, just more than a proton (1.673 × 10^{-27} kg). Because it is neutral, it is not repelled by nuclei or electrons, so it is very penetrating.', '<b>Isotopes</b> (Soddy): atoms of the same element with the same number of protons and electrons but <b>different numbers of neutrons</b>, so different masses but the same chemistry.']) },
  { id: 'p8nu15', dp: '8.2.2', t: 'exam', q: 'Long response (6 marks): Assess the experimental evidence for the changes to the model of the atom proposed by Rutherford and Chadwick.',
    a: L(['<b>Before</b>: Thomson’s plum pudding: positive charge spread through the atom.', '<b>Rutherford</b>: the Geiger–Marsden experiment showed most alpha particles pass through gold foil but about 1 in 8000 bounce back. This could only happen with a tiny, dense, positive nucleus and mostly empty space. His predictions of scattering at each angle matched Geiger’s measurements: strong, quantitative evidence.', '<b>Chadwick</b>: beryllium bombarded with alpha particles gave a neutral radiation that knocked 5 MeV protons out of paraffin. Conservation of energy and momentum ruled out gamma rays and gave a neutral particle of about proton mass: the <b>neutron</b>. This explained the ‘missing’ mass of nuclei and isotopes.', '<b>Assessment</b>: both changes were based on reproducible experiments and quantitative analysis, so the evidence is strong; the nuclear model with protons and neutrons is still part of today’s model.']) },
  { id: 'p8nu16', dp: '8.2.2', t: 'prac', q: 'Evaluate rolling table tennis balls at a hidden golf ball as a model of the Geiger–Marsden experiment.',
    a: L(['<b>Strengths</b>: most balls miss the small target and roll straight on, a few hit it and bounce off at large angles, so it shows that a small, concentrated target explains rare large deflections. Repeating many times gives statistics.', '<b>Weaknesses</b>: balls deflect only by contact, while alpha particles are repelled electrostatically without touching; it is one 2D target, not many layers of atoms; the target-to-‘atom’ size ratio is far larger than the real nucleus-to-atom ratio (1 : 25 000); the golf ball is not charged.', '<b>Judgement</b>: useful to show the idea of a small, dense nucleus, but not the mechanism (Coulomb repulsion) or the real scale.']) }
]});

/* ================= Inquiry Question 8.3: Quantum Mechanical Nature of the Atom ================= */
DECKS.push({ id: 'phys-m8-bo', fa: 'fa3', title: 'Bohr, hydrogen spectra and the Rydberg equation', dps: ['8.3.1', '8.3.2', '8.3.3'], cards: [
  { id: 'p8bo01', dp: '8.3.1', t: 'def', q: 'Distinguish <b>classical physics</b> from <b>quantum mechanics</b>.',
    a: L(['<b>Classical physics</b> (NESA): physics as understood before quantum physics and relativity; the rules established before the end of the 19th century. It works well at everyday (macroscopic) scales.', '<b>Quantum mechanics</b>: describes nature at the scale of atoms and subatomic particles, where classical physics fails.', 'Key quantum ideas: quantities such as energy come in <b>discrete values</b> (quantisation); objects have both particle and wave properties (wave–particle duality); there are limits to how precisely quantities can be known (the uncertainty principle).']) },
  { id: 'p8bo02', dp: '8.3.1', t: 'con', q: 'What were the two main limitations of Rutherford’s model of the atom?',
    a: OL(['<b>Stability</b>: an orbiting electron is accelerating, and Maxwell’s classical theory says accelerating charges radiate EM waves. The electron should lose energy and spiral into the nucleus very quickly, so atoms should be unstable. They are not.', '<b>Line spectra</b>: excited electrons returning towards the nucleus should give a <b>continuous</b> spectrum, but excited hydrogen emits only a few <b>exact</b> wavelengths.']) + N('A model must explain observations, so these limitations meant it had to be changed.') },
  { id: 'p8bo03', dp: '8.3.2', t: 'rec', q: 'What is the <b>Balmer series</b>? Give its four visible wavelengths.',
    a: L(['The lines of the hydrogen emission spectrum in the <b>visible</b> region, produced when electrons fall to <b>n = 2</b>.', 'Visible lines: <b>656 nm</b> (red), <b>486 nm</b> (cyan/blue-green), <b>434 nm</b> (blue), <b>410 nm</b> (violet).', 'The lines get closer together towards the ultraviolet end, converging at about 365 nm.', 'Balmer (1885), a school teacher, found an equation that fitted these wavelengths by trial and error; it also predicted ultraviolet lines that were later found.']), v: 'p8spectra' },
  { id: 'p8bo04', dp: '8.3.2', t: 'prac', q: 'How is the hydrogen emission spectrum (Balmer series) produced and observed in the lab?',
    a: L(['A sealed glass discharge tube contains hydrogen gas at <b>low pressure</b>.', 'A large potential difference (several kV) across electrodes in the tube excites the atoms, and it glows pinkish-purple.', 'View the light in a darkened room through a <b>spectroscope</b> (diffraction grating or prism) to see four separate coloured lines on a dark background.']) },
  { id: 'p8bo05', dp: '8.3.3', t: 'rec', q: 'State Bohr’s three postulates (1913).',
    a: OL(['Electrons exist in <b>stable, circular orbits</b> (stationary states) and <b>do not emit radiation</b> while in them.', 'Electrons <b>absorb or emit a specific quantum of energy</b> when moving between stable energy levels: ΔE = E_{i} − E_{f} = hf = ' + F('hc', 'λ') + '.', 'An electron’s angular momentum is <b>quantised</b>: mvr = ' + F('nh', '2π') + ', where n is the energy level (n = 1 innermost).']) + N('You need to remember these. The third is not on your data sheet.') },
  { id: 'p8bo06', dp: '8.3.3', t: 'con', q: 'Explain how Bohr’s model accounts for the hydrogen emission spectrum, using the law of conservation of energy.',
    a: L(['Electrons can only occupy fixed energy levels, so the energy differences between levels are fixed.', 'Energy from the discharge excites electrons to higher levels; they then drop to lower levels.', 'Energy is conserved: the energy lost by the electron is carried away by one photon, ΔE = hf = hc/λ.', 'Only certain energy differences exist, so only <b>specific wavelengths</b> are emitted: a line spectrum.', 'Drops to n = 2 give the visible Balmer lines, e.g. 3 → 2 gives 656 nm. To move to a higher level the electron must <b>absorb</b> exactly that energy.']), v: 'p8hlevels' },
  { id: 'p8bo07', dp: '8.3.3', t: 'eq', q: 'State the <b>Rydberg equation</b> and how to use it.',
    a: E(F('1', 'λ') + ' = R(' + F('1', 'n_{f}^2') + ' − ' + F('1', 'n_{i}^2') + ')') + L(['R = 1.097 × 10^7 m^{-1} (data sheet); n_{f} = final level; n_{i} = initial level.', 'For the Balmer (visible) series, n_{f} = <b>2</b>.', 'It gives 1/λ: remember to <b>invert</b> to get λ.', 'For ΔE use E = hf = hc/λ; 1 eV = 1.602 × 10^{-19} J.']) },
  { id: 'p8bo08', dp: '8.3.3', t: 'rec', q: 'Name the hydrogen spectral series, the level each ends on, and its region of the spectrum.',
    a: P('Bohr’s model predicted series beyond the Balmer series, and they were found where predicted.'), v: 'p8series' },
  { id: 'p8bo09', dp: '8.3.3', t: 'calc', q: 'Calculate the wavelength of light emitted when an electron in hydrogen falls from n = 3 to n = 2. What colour is it?',
    a: E(F('1', 'λ') + ' = 1.097 × 10^7 (' + F('1', '2^2') + ' − ' + F('1', '3^2') + ') = 1.524 × 10^6 m^{-1}') + P('λ = 6.56 × 10^{-7} m = <b>656 nm</b>: <b>red</b> (H-alpha, the first Balmer line).') + N('Similarly: 4 → 2 gives 486 nm, 5 → 2 gives 434 nm, 6 → 2 gives 410 nm.') },
  { id: 'p8bo10', dp: '8.3.3', t: 'calc', q: 'NESA 2017 (4 marks): With reference to Bohr’s postulates, explain how the 434.0 nm line of hydrogen is produced. Support your answer with a calculation.',
    a: L(['Postulate 1: electrons only occupy fixed energy levels and don’t radiate there. Postulate 2: an electron moving to a lower level emits a photon of energy ΔE = hf = hc/λ.', 'The line is visible, so it is a Balmer line with n_{f} = 2.']) + E(F('1', '434.0 × 10^{-9}') + ' = 1.097 × 10^7 (' + F('1', '4') + ' − ' + F('1', 'n_{i}^2') + ')  →  n_{i}^2 = 25.0') + P('<b>n_{i} = 5</b>: the line is produced when electrons fall from n = 5 to n = 2, each emitting a photon of energy hc/λ = 4.58 × 10^{-19} J.') },
  { id: 'p8bo11', dp: '8.3.3', t: 'calc', q: 'NESA 2016 (3 marks): An electron in hydrogen emits a photon of energy 4.089 × 10^{-19} J in a transition to n = 2. Find the initial energy level.',
    a: OL(['λ = ' + F('hc', 'E') + ' = ' + F('6.626 × 10^{-34} × 3.00 × 10^8', '4.089 × 10^{-19}') + ' = 4.861 × 10^{-7} m', F('1', '4.861 × 10^{-7}') + ' = 1.097 × 10^7 (' + F('1', '4') + ' − ' + F('1', 'n_{i}^2') + '), so ' + F('1', 'n_{i}^2') + ' = 0.0625', '<b>n_{i} = 4</b> (it must be a whole number)']) },
  { id: 'p8bo12', dp: '8.3.3', t: 'calc', q: 'An electron returns from n = ∞ to n = 1 in hydrogen. Find the wavelength, frequency and energy (J and eV) of the photon. What is the significance?',
    a: L(['1/λ = R(1/1 − 0) = R, so λ = 1/(1.097 × 10^7) = <b>9.12 × 10^{-8} m</b> (91 nm, ultraviolet)', 'f = c/λ = <b>3.29 × 10^{15} Hz</b>', 'E = hf = <b>2.18 × 10^{-18} J</b> = 2.18 × 10^{-18} ÷ 1.602 × 10^{-19} = <b>13.6 eV</b>', 'This is hydrogen’s <b>ionisation energy</b>. The experimentally measured value is 13.6 eV, so Bohr’s model correctly predicted it: strong support for the model.']) },
  { id: 'p8bo13', dp: '8.3.3', t: 'calc', q: 'Hydrogen’s levels are n = 1: −13.59 eV, n = 2: −3.40 eV, n = 3: −1.51 eV, n = 4: −0.85 eV, n = 5: −0.54 eV. Identify the transitions for the 656, 486 and 434 nm lines. Why can’t a school spectroscope show a predicted 122 nm line?',
    a: L(['E = hc/λ: 656 nm = 1.89 eV (n = 3 → 2); 486 nm = 2.55 eV (4 → 2); 434 nm = 2.86 eV (5 → 2).', '122 nm = 10.2 eV, the n = 2 → 1 transition (first Lyman line).', 'It is in the <b>ultraviolet</b>, outside the visible range (about 400–750 nm), so it cannot be seen with a school spectroscope.']) + N('Your booklet’s answer page says 122 nm is infrared; it is ultraviolet.') },
  { id: 'p8bo14', dp: '8.3.3', t: 'calc', q: 'Calculate the energy, in eV, of the photon emitted at 656.2 nm.',
    a: E('E = ' + F('hc', 'λ') + ' = ' + F('6.626 × 10^{-34} × 3.00 × 10^8', '656.2 × 10^{-9}') + ' = 3.03 × 10^{-19} J') + P('E = 3.03 × 10^{-19} ÷ 1.602 × 10^{-19} = <b>1.89 eV</b>') },
  { id: 'p8bo15', dp: '8.3.1', t: 'exam', q: 'NESA 2015 (4 marks): Explain why the spectroscope was important in the development of the Bohr model of the atom.',
    a: L(['Light from low-pressure hydrogen in a discharge tube, viewed through a spectroscope, is not continuous: it shows only a few <b>exact wavelengths</b> (the Balmer lines).', 'The Rutherford model could not explain this; it predicted a continuous spectrum.', 'Exact wavelengths mean exact photon energies (E = hc/λ), so Bohr proposed that electrons occupy <b>fixed energy levels</b> and emit a photon when they move between them.', 'Without the spectroscope’s precise measurements of the hydrogen lines (and Balmer’s and Rydberg’s equations fitted to them), Bohr could not have developed or tested his model.']) },
  { id: 'p8bo16', dp: '8.3.1', t: 'rec', q: 'List the limitations of Bohr’s model of the atom.',
    a: L(['It only works fully for <b>hydrogen</b> (and one-electron ions such as He⁺ and Li^{2+}); it cannot predict spectra of multi-electron atoms.', 'It cannot explain the different <b>intensities and widths</b> of spectral lines (some bright and sharp, others faint and diffuse).', 'It cannot explain the <b>Zeeman effect</b>: lines splitting into several lines in a strong magnetic field.', 'It cannot explain <b>hyperfine structure</b>: fine splitting of lines seen with better spectroscopes.', 'It does not explain <b>why</b> electrons in stable orbits don’t radiate, or why angular momentum is quantised.', 'It is an untidy mix of classical physics (circular orbits, forces) and quantum ideas (fixed energies).']) },
  { id: 'p8bo17', dp: '8.3.1', t: 'exam', q: 'NESA 2014 (6 marks): Assess the effectiveness of the Bohr–Rutherford model of the atom in accounting for experimental observations.',
    a: L(['<b>Strengths</b>: the dense, positive nucleus explains Geiger–Marsden alpha scattering; fixed energy levels explain hydrogen’s line spectrum, and the Rydberg equation predicts its wavelengths, including UV and IR series found later; it correctly predicts hydrogen’s ionisation energy (13.6 eV).', '<b>Weaknesses</b>: it fails for atoms with more than one electron; it cannot explain different line intensities, the Zeeman effect or hyperfine lines; it gives no reason why orbiting electrons don’t radiate; it mixes classical and quantum physics.', '<b>Judgement</b>: very effective for hydrogen and an important step towards quantum theory, but limited, so it was replaced by the quantum mechanical (de Broglie and Schrödinger) model.']) },
  { id: 'p8bo18', dp: '8.3.1', t: 'con', q: 'Why does Bohr’s model work for He⁺ and Li^{2+}, but not for neutral helium?',
    a: L(['He⁺ and Li^{2+} have only <b>one electron</b>, like hydrogen, so Bohr’s single-electron treatment works (with a larger nuclear charge).', 'Neutral helium has two electrons that repel and shield each other; Bohr’s model has no way to include these electron–electron interactions.']) }
]});

DECKS.push({ id: 'phys-m8-db', fa: 'fa3', title: 'Matter waves and the quantum atom', dps: ['8.3.1', '8.3.4', '8.3.5'], cards: [
  { id: 'p8db01', dp: '8.3.4', t: 'con', q: 'What is <b>diffraction</b>, and how did the Braggs use X-ray diffraction?',
    a: L(['<b>Diffraction</b>: the spreading out of wavefronts as they pass through a small aperture or around an obstacle; a property of <b>waves</b>.', 'The Braggs (1912) fired X-rays (wavelength about 10^{-10} m) at crystals. X-rays reflected from regularly spaced planes of atoms <b>interfered</b> constructively and destructively, giving a pattern of spots.', 'Constructive interference when nλ = 2d sin θ (Bragg’s law), so the pattern revealed the arrangement and spacing of atoms in the crystal.']) },
  { id: 'p8db02', dp: '8.3.4', t: 'eq', q: 'State de Broglie’s hypothesis (1924) and his equation.',
    a: P('<b>All moving matter has wave-like properties</b>, with a wavelength inversely proportional to its momentum:') + E('λ = ' + F('h', 'p') + ' = ' + F('h', 'mv')) + L(['λ = de Broglie wavelength (m); h = 6.626 × 10^{-34} J s; m = mass (kg); v = velocity (m s^{-1})', 'His reasoning: light (a wave) had been shown to behave like particles (the photoelectric effect), so by symmetry, particles should behave like waves.']) },
  { id: 'p8db03', dp: '8.3.4', t: 'con', q: 'Why was de Broglie’s hypothesis not readily accepted at first?',
    a: L(['It was a theoretical idea, acceptable mathematically, but with <b>no experimental evidence</b> that matter could behave as a wave.', 'Scientists expect new ideas to be supported by evidence (and verified by other teams).', 'It was accepted after Davisson and Germer (and G. P. Thomson) observed <b>electron diffraction</b> in 1927.']) },
  { id: 'p8db04', dp: '8.3.4', t: 'calc', q: 'Calculate the de Broglie wavelength of a 120 g ball at 10 m s^{-1}. Why don’t we notice the wave nature of everyday objects?',
    a: E('λ = ' + F('h', 'mv') + ' = ' + F('6.626 × 10^{-34}', '0.120 × 10') + ' = <b>5.52 × 10^{-34} m</b>') + L(['At 40 m s^{-1}: 1.38 × 10^{-34} m. λ ∝ 1/v and λ ∝ 1/m.', 'This wavelength is far smaller than any gap or obstacle (even a nucleus), so no diffraction can be observed.', 'Only tiny masses (electrons, neutrons) have wavelengths comparable to atomic spacings.']) },
  { id: 'p8db05', dp: '8.3.4', t: 'calc', q: 'A proton is accelerated from rest through 50.0 V. Calculate its de Broglie wavelength, and the frequency of X-rays with the same wavelength.',
    a: OL(['qV = ½mv^2: v = √(2 × 1.602 × 10^{-19} × 50.0 ÷ 1.673 × 10^{-27}) = 9.79 × 10^4 m s^{-1}', 'λ = ' + F('h', 'mv') + ' = ' + F('6.626 × 10^{-34}', '1.673 × 10^{-27} × 9.79 × 10^4') + ' = <b>4.05 × 10^{-12} m</b>', 'X-rays travel at c: f = ' + F('c', 'λ') + ' = ' + F('3.00 × 10^8', '4.05 × 10^{-12}') + ' = <b>7.41 × 10^{19} Hz</b>']) + N('Proton charge: same size as the electron’s (1.602 × 10^{-19} C) but positive.') },
  { id: 'p8db06', dp: '8.3.4', t: 'calc', q: 'Electrons travel at 2.0 × 10^7 m s^{-1}. Calculate their momentum and de Broglie wavelength.',
    a: OL(['p = mv = 9.109 × 10^{-31} × 2.0 × 10^7 = <b>1.8 × 10^{-23} kg m s^{-1}</b>', 'λ = ' + F('h', 'p') + ' = ' + F('6.626 × 10^{-34}', '1.822 × 10^{-23}') + ' = <b>3.6 × 10^{-11} m</b>']) + N('Your booklet’s answer page gives 4.0 × 10^{-11} m; recalculating gives 3.6 × 10^{-11} m.') },
  { id: 'p8db07', dp: '8.3.4', t: 'calc', q: 'NESA 2015: A beam of neutrons has a wavelength of 0.20 nm. Calculate their speed, and explain why such a beam is useful for studying the structure of materials.',
    a: E('v = ' + F('h', 'mλ') + ' = ' + F('6.626 × 10^{-34}', '1.675 × 10^{-27} × 0.20 × 10^{-9}') + ' = <b>2.0 × 10^3 m s^{-1}</b>') + L(['Moving neutrons have a wave nature; 0.20 nm is similar to the spacing between atoms, so the beam <b>diffracts</b> from the atomic structure, revealing the arrangement of atoms.', 'Neutrons are uncharged, so they are not repelled by electrons or nuclei and penetrate deep into the material.']) },
  { id: 'p8db08', dp: '8.3.4', t: 'prac', q: 'Describe the Davisson and Germer experiment (1927), its result and its significance.',
    a: L(['They fired a beam of <b>electrons</b> at a <b>nickel</b> surface in a vacuum and measured how many were scattered at different angles.', 'Air leaked in and oxidised the nickel; heating it to remove the oxide re-formed it into large single crystals.', 'Afterwards they found <b>peaks in intensity</b> at certain angles: a <b>diffraction pattern</b>, like X-ray diffraction from crystals.', 'Diffraction is produced by constructive and destructive interference of <b>waves</b>, so moving electrons behave as waves. The measured wavelength matched λ = h/mv.', 'This confirmed de Broglie’s hypothesis (also confirmed by G. P. Thomson the same year), showing matter waves are real, not just mathematics.']) },
  { id: 'p8db09', dp: '8.3.4', t: 'con', q: 'How does de Broglie’s model explain the stable orbits in Bohr’s atom?',
    a: L(['Electrons in atoms are <b>standing waves</b> around the nucleus rather than orbiting particles.', 'An orbit is stable only if its circumference fits a <b>whole number of electron wavelengths</b>: 2πr = nλ. Other orbits would cancel out by destructive interference.', 'A standing wave does not propagate and is not a moving (accelerating) charge, so it <b>does not radiate</b>: this explains Bohr’s first postulate.']) + E('2πr = nλ  and  λ = ' + F('h', 'mv') + '  →  mvr = ' + F('nh', '2π')) + P('This <b>derives Bohr’s third postulate</b>, which Bohr had simply assumed.'), v: 'p8waves' },
  { id: 'p8db10', dp: '8.3.4', t: 'exam', q: 'An electron is in the third excited state (n = 4) of an atom. Sketch the de Broglie standing wave for this level.',
    a: L(['The ground state is n = 1, so the third excited state is n = 4.', 'Draw <b>four complete wavelengths</b> around the circular orbit, with nodes (no motion) and antinodes (maximum motion).']), v: 'p8wave4' },
  { id: 'p8db11', dp: '8.3.4', t: 'exam', q: 'NESA 2013 (6 marks): How did de Broglie use existing concepts and ideas to come up with new interpretations that increased our understanding of the structure of matter?',
    a: L(['<b>Before</b>: Bohr’s model had electrons in stable orbits that did not radiate, but gave no reason why, and simply assumed angular momentum was quantised.', '<b>Existing idea</b>: light showed both wave and particle behaviour (Planck’s quanta, Einstein’s photons). De Broglie proposed the reverse: moving matter has a wave nature, λ = h/mv.', '<b>New interpretation</b>: electrons are standing waves; only orbits with circumference = nλ are stable, so energy levels are quantised and electrons don’t radiate. This derived Bohr’s third postulate (mvr = nh/2π).', '<b>After</b>: confirmed by electron diffraction (Davisson and Germer), it established wave–particle duality of matter and led to Schrödinger’s quantum mechanical model.']) + N('For ‘increased understanding’ questions, always give the ‘before’ model and the ‘after’ model.') },
  { id: 'p8db12', dp: '8.3.4', t: 'con', q: 'What does <b>wave–particle duality</b> mean? Give two examples of experimental evidence and the scientists involved.',
    a: L(['Light and matter both show some properties that can only be explained by a <b>particle</b> model and others that can only be explained by a <b>wave</b> model.', '<b>Light as particles</b>: the photoelectric effect, explained by Einstein using photons.', '<b>Matter as waves</b>: electron diffraction from nickel crystals (Davisson and Germer).']) + N('It does not mean something is either a wave or a particle; it means both models are needed to explain its behaviour.') },
  { id: 'p8db13', dp: '8.3.5', t: 'con', q: 'What did <b>Pauli</b> and <b>Heisenberg</b> contribute to the quantum model of the atom?',
    a: L(['<b>Pauli exclusion principle</b> (1925): no two electrons in an atom can have the same set of four quantum numbers (energy level, orbital shape, orbital orientation and spin). So each orbital holds at most two electrons, with opposite spins. This explains the arrangement of electrons and the structure of the periodic table.', '<b>Heisenberg uncertainty principle</b> (1927): the more precisely we know a particle’s position, the less precisely we can know its momentum (and velocity), and vice versa. Measuring one quantity disturbs the other.', 'Together they showed electrons cannot be pictured as particles in exact orbits, leading to the electron cloud model.']) },
  { id: 'p8db14', dp: '8.3.5', t: 'con', q: 'Analyse Schrödinger’s contribution to the current model of the atom.',
    a: L(['Schrödinger (1926) developed a <b>wave equation</b> describing the wave behaviour of electrons mathematically, for atoms more complex than hydrogen.', 'He interpreted the wave as giving the <b>probability</b> of finding an electron at a location, not a definite path.', 'Solutions give <b>orbitals</b> (s, p, d, f): 3D regions where an electron is likely to be found, replacing Bohr’s circular orbits.', 'This is the current <b>quantum mechanical (electron cloud) model</b>: the denser the cloud, the more likely the electron is there. It also explains sub-energy levels.']) + N('Your booklet gives 1925; his equation was published in 1926.') },
  { id: 'p8db15', dp: '8.3.5', t: 'exam', q: '2021 HSC Q29 (5 marks): Bohr, de Broglie and Schrödinger each proposed a model for the structure of the atom. How does the nature of the electron differ in each model?',
    a: L(['<b>Bohr</b>: the electron is a <b>particle</b> moving in a fixed circular orbit with quantised energy and angular momentum; it does not radiate while in an orbit, though no reason is given.', '<b>de Broglie</b>: the electron is a <b>standing matter wave</b> around the nucleus; only orbits whose circumference fits a whole number of wavelengths (2πr = nλ) are allowed, which explains quantisation and stability.', '<b>Schrödinger</b>: the electron is described by a <b>wave function</b> giving the <b>probability</b> of finding it in a region (an orbital or electron cloud); it has no definite path.']) },
  { id: 'p8db16', dp: '8.3.1', t: 'exam', q: 'NESA (8 marks): Analyse the way scientists use observations and mathematical ideas to improve scientific models, referring to scientists who contributed to our understanding of the atom. Outline the key points.',
    a: L(['Models explain observations but are limited; an observation they cannot explain forces a change.', '<b>Rutherford</b>: alpha particles occasionally bouncing back (Geiger–Marsden) led to the nuclear model. Limitation: orbiting electrons should radiate and spiral in.', '<b>Bohr</b>: hydrogen’s line spectrum led to fixed energy levels; the Rydberg equation modelled the wavelengths mathematically. Limitation: only hydrogen; no reason for quantisation.', '<b>de Broglie</b>: the mathematical idea λ = h/mv and standing waves (2πr = nλ) explained quantisation; confirmed by Davisson and Germer’s electron diffraction.', '<b>Schrödinger</b>: a wave equation giving probability orbitals: the current model.', '<b>Relationship</b>: each model improved on the last by explaining new observations, and mathematical models made testable predictions.']) },
  { id: 'p8db17', dp: '8.3.4', t: 'con', q: 'How is de Broglie’s atomic model an improvement on Bohr’s?',
    a: L(['It <b>explains</b> why only certain orbits are stable (whole-number standing waves) rather than just assuming it.', 'It explains why electrons in stable orbits don’t radiate.', 'Bohr’s third postulate can be <b>derived</b> mathematically instead of assumed: fewer arbitrary assumptions.', 'It is not an untidy mix of classical and quantum physics, and it can be extended to more complex atoms (leading to Schrödinger’s model).']) }
]});

/* ================= Inquiry Question 8.4: Properties of the Nucleus ================= */
DECKS.push({ id: 'phys-m8-ra', fa: 'fa4', title: 'Nuclear stability and radioactive decay', dps: ['8.4.1'], cards: [
  { id: 'p8ra01', dp: '8.4.1', t: 'def', q: 'What is a <b>nucleon</b>? Explain isotopic notation and how to find the number of neutrons.',
    a: L(['<b>Nucleon</b>: a particle in the nucleus, i.e. a proton or a neutron.', `Notation ${NU('A', 'Z', 'X')}: A = <b>mass number</b> (number of nucleons) at the top left; Z = <b>atomic number</b> (number of protons) at the bottom left; any ion charge goes at the top right.`, '<b>N = A − Z</b> (number of neutrons).', `e.g. ${NU(23, 11, 'Na')}⁺: 11 protons, 12 neutrons, 10 electrons (it has lost one).`]) + N('Proton: +1.602 × 10^{-19} C, 1.673 × 10^{-27} kg (quarks uud). Neutron: 0 C, 1.675 × 10^{-27} kg (udd).') },
  { id: 'p8ra02', dp: '8.4.1', t: 'calc', q: 'Two protons in a nucleus are 1.3 × 10^{-15} m apart. Compare the electrostatic repulsion with the gravitational attraction. What does this show?',
    a: OL(['F_{E} = ' + F('1', '4πε_0') + F('q^2', 'r^2') + ' = 8.98 × 10^9 × ' + F('(1.602 × 10^{-19})^2', '(1.3 × 10^{-15})^2') + ' = <b>136 N</b>', 'F_{G} = ' + F('Gm^2', 'r^2') + ' = 6.67 × 10^{-11} × ' + F('(1.673 × 10^{-27})^2', '(1.3 × 10^{-15})^2') + ' = <b>1.1 × 10^{-34} N</b>', 'Ratio ≈ <b>1.2 × 10^{36} : 1</b>']) + P('Gravity cannot possibly hold the nucleus together against this repulsion, so another, much stronger attractive force must act: the <b>strong nuclear force</b>.') },
  { id: 'p8ra03', dp: '8.4.1', t: 'rec', q: 'List the properties of the <b>strong nuclear force</b>.',
    a: L(['Acts between <b>all nucleons</b> (p–p, n–n and p–n), regardless of charge.', '<b>Very short range</b>: attractive at about 1 fm (10^{-15} m), dropping to zero by about 3 fm.', '<b>Repulsive</b> at very small separations, which stops nucleons merging.', 'Far stronger than the electrostatic force at nuclear distances (your booklet: about 100 times, and 10^{38} times stronger than gravity).', 'First described by Hideki Yukawa (1935).']), v: 'p8forces3' },
  { id: 'p8ra04', dp: '8.4.1', t: 'con', q: 'Why do larger nuclei need proportionally more neutrons to be stable?',
    a: L(['The strong force only acts between neighbouring nucleons, but electrostatic repulsion acts between <b>every pair of protons</b> across the whole nucleus.', 'As Z increases, total repulsion grows faster than the attraction from neighbours.', 'Extra neutrons add strong-force attraction without adding repulsion, and spread the protons further apart (‘nuclear glue’).', 'So the stable neutron : proton ratio rises from about 1 for light nuclei to about 1.5 for lead.']) },
  { id: 'p8ra05', dp: '8.4.1', t: 'con', q: 'What three ideas are used to predict nuclear stability?',
    a: OL(['<b>Neutron : proton ratio</b> and the <b>band of stability</b>: stable nuclides plot in a narrow band on a graph of N against Z; too many or too few neutrons makes a nucleus unstable.', '<b>Odd/even numbers</b>: even–even nuclei (even Z and even N) are most stable (168 stable nuclides); odd–odd are least stable (only 4).', '<b>Magic numbers</b>: nuclei with 2, 8, 20, 28, 50 or 82 protons, or 2, 8, 20, 28, 50, 82 or 126 neutrons (filled nuclear shells), are especially stable; ‘doubly magic’ nuclei most of all.']) + N('These are general guidelines with exceptions (e.g. neodymium-147 is radioactive though it lies in the band).') },
  { id: 'p8ra06', dp: '8.4.1', t: 'con', q: 'How does a nuclide’s position relative to the band of stability predict its type of decay?',
    a: L(['<b>Above the band</b> (too many neutrons): <b>β⁻ decay</b>. A neutron becomes a proton, lowering n/p.', '<b>Below the band</b> (too few neutrons): <b>β⁺ decay</b> or electron capture. A proton becomes a neutron, raising n/p.', '<b>Z > 82</b> (heavier than lead): <b>α decay</b>. No number of neutrons can make the attraction beat the repulsion, so the nucleus must get smaller.']), v: 'p8band' },
  { id: 'p8ra07', dp: '8.4.1', t: 'exam', q: `Predict whether each is stable or unstable, and the likely decay: ${NU(239, 94, 'Pu')}, ${NU(4, 2, 'He')}, ${NU(118, 54, 'Xe')}, ${NU(40, 20, 'Ca')}, ${NU(232, 90, 'Th')}.`,
    a: L([`${NU(239, 94, 'Pu')}: <b>unstable, α decay</b> (Z > 82).`, `${NU(4, 2, 'He')}: <b>stable</b> (magic numbers: 2 protons, 2 neutrons; even–even).`, `${NU(118, 54, 'Xe')}: <b>unstable, β⁺ decay</b>: n/p = 64/54 ≈ 1.19, too low for Z = 54, so below the band.`, `${NU(40, 20, 'Ca')}: <b>stable</b> (doubly magic: 20 protons, 20 neutrons).`, `${NU(232, 90, 'Th')}: <b>unstable, α decay</b> (Z > 82).`]) },
  { id: 'p8ra08', dp: '8.4.1', t: 'def', q: 'Define <b>transmutation</b> and <b>radioisotope</b>. How is ionisation different from radioactive decay?',
    a: L(['<b>Transmutation</b>: the change of one chemical element into another by nuclear decay or by bombardment (e.g. with alpha particles or neutrons).', '<b>Radioisotope</b>: an isotope with an unstable nucleus that emits radiation as it decays to a more stable form (daughters have greater binding energy per nucleon). Used in medicine and research.', '<b>Ionisation</b> is the removal of an electron from an atom, forming an ion: an electron process, not a nuclear change.']) },
  { id: 'p8ra09', dp: '8.4.1', t: 'rec', q: 'Compare alpha, beta (β⁻ and β⁺) and gamma radiation.',
    a: P('Penetrating power and ionising power are inversely related: alpha ionises most strongly, so it loses its energy fastest and travels least far.'), v: 'p8decaytbl' },
  { id: 'p8ra10', dp: '8.4.1', t: 'rec', q: 'What two conservation laws balance a nuclear equation? List the conventions for writing one.',
    a: L(['<b>Conservation of nucleon number</b>: top (mass) numbers add up to the same on both sides.', '<b>Conservation of charge</b>: bottom (atomic/charge) numbers add up to the same on both sides.', `Write the actual particle, not α or β: ${AL}, ${BM}, ${BP}.`, `Gamma is written γ or ${GA} because it is EM radiation, not a particle with mass. An excited nucleus has an asterisk, e.g. ${NU(99, 43, 'Tc')}*.`, `Always include the antineutrino ${NB} with β⁻ and the neutrino ν with β⁺.`, 'The parent goes on the left of the arrow; the order of products doesn’t matter.']) },
  { id: 'p8ra11', dp: '8.4.1', t: 'eq', q: 'Write nuclear equations for: uranium-238 alpha decay; thorium-234 beta-minus decay; carbon-10 beta-plus decay; technetium-99m gamma emission.',
    a: E(`${NU(238, 92, 'U')} → ${NU(234, 90, 'Th')} + ${AL}`) + E(`${NU(234, 90, 'Th')} → ${NU(234, 91, 'Pa')} + ${BM} + ${NB}`) + E(`${NU(10, 6, 'C')} → ${NU(10, 5, 'B')} + ${BP} + ν`) + E(`${NU(99, 43, 'Tc')}* → ${NU(99, 43, 'Tc')} + ${GA}`) + N('If a question just says ‘beta decay’, assume beta-minus.') },
  { id: 'p8ra12', dp: '8.4.1', t: 'eq', q: 'Write the equations for: polonium-214 emits an alpha particle; thallium-210 emits a beta-minus particle; bismuth-212 emits a positron; sodium-24 emits a beta particle.',
    a: E(`${NU(214, 84, 'Po')} → ${NU(210, 82, 'Pb')} + ${AL}`) + E(`${NU(210, 81, 'Tl')} → ${NU(210, 82, 'Pb')} + ${BM} + ${NB}`) + E(`${NU(212, 83, 'Bi')} → ${NU(212, 82, 'Pb')} + ${BP} + ν`) + E(`${NU(24, 11, 'Na')} → ${NU(24, 12, 'Mg')} + ${BM} + ${NB}`) },
  { id: 'p8ra13', dp: '8.4.1', t: 'eq', q: 'Find the parent: (a) an element emits an alpha particle to form lead-208; (b) an element emits a beta particle to form oxygen-13.',
    a: E(`(a)  ${NU(212, 84, 'Po')} → ${NU(208, 82, 'Pb')} + ${AL}`) + E(`(b)  ${NU(13, 7, 'N')} → ${NU(13, 8, 'O')} + ${BM} + ${NB}`) + N('Work backwards: add the emitted particle’s numbers to the daughter’s, then find the element from Z on the periodic table.') },
  { id: 'p8ra14', dp: '8.4.1', t: 'eq', q: 'What happens inside the nucleus in β⁻ and β⁺ decay? Write both equations.',
    a: L([`<b>β⁻</b>: a neutron changes into a proton, emitting an electron and an antineutrino: ${NE} → ${NU(1, 1, 'p')} + ${BM} + ${NB}. Z increases by 1; A is unchanged.`, `<b>β⁺</b>: a proton changes into a neutron, emitting a positron and a neutrino: ${NU(1, 1, 'p')} → ${NE} + ${BP} + ν. Z decreases by 1; A is unchanged.`, 'In quark terms, β⁻ is a down quark becoming an up quark (udd → uud).']) },
  { id: 'p8ra15', dp: '8.4.1', t: 'con', q: 'What is a <b>decay series</b>? Describe the uranium-238 series.',
    a: L(['When a radioisotope decays, its daughter is often also unstable, so decays continue until a stable nucleus forms. This chain is a decay series. It is why some short-lived radioactive elements exist in nature.', '<b>Uranium-238 → lead-206</b> in 14 steps: 8 alpha and 6 beta-minus decays.', 'U-238 →α Th-234 →β Pa-234 →β U-234 →α Th-230 →α Ra-226 →α Rn-222 →α Po-218 →α Pb-214 →β Bi-214 →β Po-214 →α Pb-210 →β Bi-210 →β Po-210 →α Pb-206.']) + N('Check: mass falls by 8 × 4 = 32 (238 → 206); Z falls by 16 − 6 = 10 (92 → 82).') },
  { id: 'p8ra16', dp: '8.4.1', t: 'exam', q: 'Radiation from polonium travels left to right into a magnetic field directed into the page. Three paths result: P curves up, Q goes straight, R curves down more tightly. Identify each and explain why R is the tightest curve.',
    a: L(['<b>P = alpha</b> (positive): by the right-hand slap rule (v to the right, B into the page) the force F = qvB is upwards.', '<b>Q = gamma</b>: no charge, so no magnetic force; it travels straight.', '<b>R = beta-minus</b> (negative): the force is reversed, so it curves downwards.', '<b>R is tighter</b>: r = mv/(qB). A beta particle’s mass is about 1/7000 of an alpha’s, while its charge is only half as big, so its radius is much smaller (it accelerates much more for the force it feels).']) }
]});

DECKS.push({ id: 'phys-m8-hl', fa: 'fa4', title: 'Half-life and decay calculations', dps: ['8.4.2'], cards: [
  { id: 'p8hl01', dp: '8.4.2', t: 'con', q: 'Why can we predict how much of a sample decays, even though decay is random?',
    a: L(['The decay of an individual nucleus is a <b>random</b> event and can’t be predicted.', 'But a sample contains an enormous number of nuclei, each with the same probability of decaying per second.', 'So the number decaying in a short time is proportional to the number present: ΔN = −λNΔt (the minus sign shows N decreasing).', 'This gives <b>exponential decay</b>, with a constant half-life.']), v: 'p8decay' },
  { id: 'p8hl02', dp: '8.4.2', t: 'eq', q: 'State the radioactive decay law and the decay constant equation, with the meaning of each symbol.',
    a: E('N_{t} = N_0e^{-λt}    λ = ' + F('ln 2', 't_{½}')) + L(['N_0 = initial number of nuclei (at t = 0); N_{t} = number left after time t', 'λ = decay constant (per unit time; a larger λ means faster decay)', 't_{½} = half-life: the time for half of the parent nuclei to decay']) + N('Use the ln (natural log) button, not log. Any units are fine if consistent: if t is in days, λ must be per day. Masses, activities, count rates or percentages can replace N.') },
  { id: 'p8hl03', dp: '8.4.2', t: 'eq', q: 'Derive λ = ln 2 / t_{½} from the decay law.',
    a: OL(['At t = t_{½}, N_{t} = ½N_0, so ½N_0 = N_0e^{-λt_{½}}', 'Cancel N_0: ½ = e^{-λt_{½}}', 'Take natural logs: ln(½) = −λt_{½}, and ln(½) = −ln 2', 'So ln 2 = λt_{½}, giving λ = ln 2 / t_{½}']) + N('The longer the half-life, the smaller the decay constant and the more slowly the isotope decays.') },
  { id: 'p8hl04', dp: '8.4.2', t: 'calc', q: 'A source’s activity drops from 600 to 100 counts per minute in 6 hours. Find the decay constant and half-life.',
    a: OL(['100 = 600e^{-6λ}, so e^{-6λ} = 0.1667', 'ln(0.1667) = −6λ, so −1.792 = −6λ', 'λ = <b>0.30 h^{-1}</b>', 't_{½} = ' + F('ln 2', 'λ') + ' = ' + F('0.693', '0.2986') + ' = <b>2.3 hours</b>']) + N('λ is in per hour, so the half-life comes out in hours.') },
  { id: 'p8hl05', dp: '8.4.2', t: 'calc', q: 'Radon-212 has a half-life of 23 minutes. A sample has 128 g. How much remains after 23 minutes, 46 minutes and 1 hour?',
    a: L(['23 min (one half-life): <b>64 g</b>', '46 min (two half-lives): <b>32 g</b>', '1 hour: λ = ln 2/23 = 0.03014 min^{-1}; N = 128e^{-0.03014 × 60} = <b>21 g</b> (2.61 half-lives)']) },
  { id: 'p8hl06', dp: '8.4.2', t: 'calc', q: 'How long does it take for 60.0 g of iodine-131 (half-life 8.040 days) to decay to 0.4 g?',
    a: OL(['λ = ' + F('ln 2', '8.040') + ' = 0.08621 day^{-1}', '0.4 = 60.0e^{-0.08621t}, so ln(' + F('0.4', '60.0') + ') = −0.08621t', 't = ' + F('5.011', '0.08621') + ' = <b>58.1 days</b>']) },
  { id: 'p8hl07', dp: '8.4.2', t: 'calc', q: 'A 144.0 mg sample decays to 6.00 mg in 20 days. Find the half-life.',
    a: OL(['6.00 = 144.0e^{-20λ}, so λ = ' + F('ln(144.0/6.00)', '20') + ' = ' + F('3.178', '20') + ' = 0.1589 day^{-1}', 't_{½} = ' + F('ln 2', '0.1589') + ' = <b>4.36 days</b>']) },
  { id: 'p8hl08', dp: '8.4.2', t: 'calc', q: 'Tritium (H-3) has a half-life of 12.26 years. (a) What fraction remains after 98 years? (b) How long until it has lost 60% of its activity?',
    a: L(['(a) N/N_0 = e^{-(ln 2/12.26) × 98} = <b>0.00392</b> (0.392%)', '(b) 40% remains: 0.40 = e^{-0.05654t}, so t = ' + F('ln(1/0.40)', '0.05654') + ' = <b>16.2 years</b>']) + N('‘Lost 60%’ means 40% remains: a common trap.') },
  { id: 'p8hl09', dp: '8.4.2', t: 'prac', q: 'How do you find a half-life from a graph of activity against time?',
    a: OL(['Plot activity (or % remaining) against time and draw a smooth curve of best fit.', 'Read the time for the activity to fall from its starting value to half that value.', 'Repeat for another halving (e.g. half to a quarter) and average the intervals for a more accurate value.']) + N('Example: carbon-14 (decays by β⁻ to nitrogen-14) has an accepted half-life of 5730 ± 40 years.') }
]});

DECKS.push({ id: 'phys-m8-fi', fa: 'fa4', title: 'Nuclear fission and reactors', dps: ['8.4.3'], cards: [
  { id: 'p8fi01', dp: '8.4.3', t: 'con', q: 'Why are neutrons better ‘bullets’ than protons for causing nuclear reactions?',
    a: L(['Neutrons are <b>uncharged</b>, so they are not repelled by the positive nucleus and pass through the electron shells unaffected.', 'Protons are repelled by the nucleus, which slows them down and makes collisions less effective, unless they are given very high energies in accelerators.', 'Their masses are almost equal, so for the same speed they carry similar momentum and energy.']) },
  { id: 'p8fi02', dp: '8.4.3', t: 'con', q: 'Outline the history of the discovery of nuclear fission (1934–1939).',
    a: L([`<b>Fermi (1934)</b>: bombarded uranium with neutrons and got radioactive β-emitting products. He thought he had made new <b>transuranic</b> elements: ${NU(238, 92, 'U')} + ${NE} → ${NU(239, 92, 'U')} → ${NU(239, 93, 'Np')} + ${BM} + ${NB}`, '<b>Hahn and Strassmann (1938)</b>: careful chemical analysis showed much <b>lighter elements</b> such as barium and krypton among the products.', '<b>Meitner and Frisch (1939)</b>: explained this: a U-235 nucleus that absorbs a neutron <b>splits</b> into two smaller nuclei, releasing 2–3 neutrons and a lot of energy. They named it nuclear fission.']) },
  { id: 'p8fi03', dp: '8.4.3', t: 'eq', q: 'Define nuclear fission and write two possible fission equations for uranium-235.',
    a: P('<b>Nuclear fission</b>: a heavy nucleus splits into two (or more) lighter nuclei, each more stable than the original, releasing neutrons and energy.') + E(`${NU(235, 92, 'U')} + ${NE} → ${NU(236, 92, 'U')} → ${NU(141, 56, 'Ba')} + ${NU(92, 36, 'Kr')} + 3 ${NE}`) + E(`${NU(235, 92, 'U')} + ${NE} → ${NU(236, 92, 'U')} → ${NU(147, 57, 'La')} + ${NU(87, 35, 'Br')} + 2 ${NE}`) + N('Check: top numbers and bottom numbers must balance. Fragments are typically around mass 140 and 90.') },
  { id: 'p8fi04', dp: '8.4.3', t: 'con', q: 'How does the speed of the neutron affect what happens when it hits a nucleus? Why is a moderator needed?',
    a: L([`<b>Fast neutrons</b> may knock a proton out (e.g. ${NU(32, 16, 'S')} + ${NE} → ${NU(32, 15, 'P')} + ${PR}) or simply bounce off.`, '<b>Slow (thermal) neutrons</b> spend longer near the nucleus and are more likely to be <b>absorbed</b>. U-235 becomes unstable U-236, which splits.', 'Fission produces fast neutrons, so a <b>moderator</b> is needed to slow them to the right energy to cause further fission.']) },
  { id: 'p8fi05', dp: '8.4.3', t: 'con', q: 'What is a <b>chain reaction</b>, and what is <b>critical mass</b>?',
    a: L(['Each fission releases 2–3 neutrons, which can cause further fissions, which release more neutrons: a self-sustaining <b>chain reaction</b>.', '<b>Critical mass</b>: the smallest mass of fissile material that can sustain a chain reaction.', 'Below critical mass, too many neutrons escape (or are absorbed by non-fissile material) before causing another fission, so the reaction <b>dies out</b>; fission only occurs at the natural decay rate.']) },
  { id: 'p8fi06', dp: '8.4.3', t: 'con', q: 'Compare controlled and uncontrolled chain reactions.',
    a: P('In a <b>controlled</b> reaction each fission leads to exactly one more on average, giving a steady energy output. In an <b>uncontrolled</b> reaction each fission causes more than one more, so the rate grows exponentially.'), v: 'p8chain' },
  { id: 'p8fi07', dp: '8.4.3', t: 'exam', q: '2012 HSC (3 marks): What are the requirements for an uncontrolled nuclear chain reaction?',
    a: L(['A mass of fissile material (e.g. U-235 or Pu-239) <b>greater than the critical mass</b>, in a suitable concentration and arrangement (highly enriched fuel).', 'Neutrons available to <b>start</b> the fission.', 'On average <b>more than one</b> neutron from each fission goes on to cause another fission, with nothing (such as control rods) absorbing the extra neutrons, so the rate increases rapidly.']) },
  { id: 'p8fi08', dp: '8.4.3', t: 'rec', q: 'Describe the structure and function of the main parts of a fission reactor.',
    a: P('Heat from fission → coolant → heat exchanger → steam → turbine → generator.'), v: 'p8reactor' },
  { id: 'p8fi09', dp: '8.4.3', t: 'con', q: 'Why is enriched uranium used in reactors? Why can’t natural uranium sustain a chain reaction?',
    a: L(['Natural uranium is only about <b>0.7% U-235</b> (fissile); the rest is mostly U-238, which is not fissile and absorbs neutrons.', 'There is too little U-235 for enough neutrons to cause further fissions, so a chain reaction can’t be sustained.', '<b>Enrichment</b> (e.g. with high-speed centrifuges) increases the U-235 proportion so the reaction can continue.']) + N('Your booklet gives 5–20% for enriched fuel and calls anything above 20% ‘weapon-grade’. Strictly, above 20% is ‘highly enriched’; weapons-grade is about 90%.') },
  { id: 'p8fi10', dp: '8.4.3', t: 'con', q: 'A reactor contains many times the critical mass of fuel. Why doesn’t it explode?',
    a: L(['The fuel is divided into separate <b>fuel rods</b>, each below critical mass, spread out among moderator material.', '<b>Control rods</b> (boron or cadmium) between them absorb excess neutrons, keeping it to one further fission per fission on average.', 'Emergency control rods can drop in under gravity, even if power fails, to stop the reaction.', 'Reactor fuel is only slightly enriched, unlike the highly enriched fuel needed for a bomb.']) },
  { id: 'p8fi11', dp: '8.4.3', t: 'con', q: 'Describe Fermi’s first nuclear reactor (Chicago, 1942).',
    a: L(['The first controlled, self-sustaining chain reaction, built as an ‘atomic pile’ in a squash court at the University of Chicago.', 'Uranium metal and uranium oxide fuel was separated by hundreds of tonnes of <b>graphite</b> blocks acting as the <b>moderator</b>.', '<b>Cadmium control rods</b> (good neutron absorbers) kept the reaction from becoming supercritical.', 'It was unshielded and produced only about 0.5 W.']) },
  { id: 'p8fi12', dp: '8.4.3', t: 'con', q: 'How did the ‘Little Boy’ atomic bomb (Hiroshima, 1945) work?',
    a: L(['It contained two pieces of enriched uranium, each <b>below critical mass</b>.', 'A chemical explosive fired one piece down a tube into the other.', 'Together they <b>exceeded the critical mass</b>, so an uncontrolled chain reaction released an enormous amount of energy almost instantly.']) },
  { id: 'p8fi13', dp: '8.4.3', t: 'con', q: 'Account for the release of energy in nuclear fission.',
    a: L(['The total mass of the fission products (fragments plus neutrons) is <b>less</b> than the mass of the original nucleus plus neutron.', 'This mass defect is converted to energy (E = Δmc^2), mostly as kinetic energy of the fragments and neutrons, plus gamma rays.', 'Equivalently: the fragments have <b>higher binding energy per nucleon</b> than uranium (they are closer to the iron peak), so energy is released when the nucleus splits.']) }
]});

DECKS.push({ id: 'phys-m8-be', fa: 'fa4', title: 'Mass defect, binding energy and fusion', dps: ['8.4.4', '8.4.5', '8.4.6'], cards: [
  { id: 'p8be01', dp: '8.4.4', t: 'con', q: 'What is the law of conservation of <b>mass–energy</b>?',
    a: L(['Einstein: mass and energy are <b>equivalent and interconvertible</b>, E = mc^2 (E in J, m in kg, c = 3.00 × 10^8 m s^{-1}).', 'Because c^2 is huge, a tiny mass is equivalent to an enormous amount of energy.', 'Mass can be converted to energy and energy to mass, but the <b>total of mass and energy</b> in a closed system stays constant.', 'So ‘energy cannot be created or destroyed’ is incomplete: mass is a form of energy.']) },
  { id: 'p8be02', dp: '8.4.6', t: 'def', q: 'What is a <b>mass defect</b>? Give its two meanings.',
    a: OL(['The difference between the mass of a nucleus and the total mass of its separate protons and neutrons (the nucleus is always <b>lighter</b>).', 'The difference between the total mass of the <b>reactants</b> and the total mass of the <b>products</b> in a nuclear reaction.']) + P('In both cases the ‘missing’ mass Δm corresponds to energy: E = Δmc^2.') },
  { id: 'p8be03', dp: '8.4.6', t: 'def', q: 'Define <b>binding energy</b> and binding energy per nucleon. How are they calculated?',
    a: L(['<b>Binding energy</b>: the energy needed to separate a nucleus completely into its individual protons and neutrons; equivalently, the energy released when the nucleus forms from them.', 'Calculated from the mass defect: ΔE = Δmc^2 (Δm in kg), or E (MeV) = Δm (u) × 931.5.', '<b>Binding energy per nucleon</b> = total binding energy ÷ mass number A. The higher it is, the more <b>stable</b> the nucleus.']) + N('1 u = 1.661 × 10^{-27} kg = 931.5 MeV/c^2 (data sheet). Your booklet’s binding energy note says 1.667 × 10^{-27} kg; use the data sheet value, 1.661 × 10^{-27} kg.') },
  { id: 'p8be04', dp: '8.4.4', t: 'con', q: 'Describe the binding energy per nucleon curve and explain how both fusion and fission release energy.',
    a: L(['Binding energy per nucleon rises steeply for light nuclei (helium-4 is unusually stable), peaks at about <b>8.8 MeV</b> near mass number 56–62 (the iron group), then falls slowly to about 7.6 MeV for uranium.', '<b>Fusion</b>: joining light nuclei (A below about 56) moves up the curve to more tightly bound nuclei: energy released.', '<b>Fission</b>: splitting heavy nuclei (A above about 56) also moves towards the peak: energy released.', 'Fusing iron or splitting light nuclei would move away from the peak and absorb energy.']), v: 'p8bind' },
  { id: 'p8be05', dp: '8.4.6', t: 'con', q: 'Why is there a peak in the binding energy per nucleon curve?',
    a: L(['For small nuclei, each added nucleon has more neighbours to attract through the strong force, so binding per nucleon increases.', 'The strong force is short-range, so once a nucleus is larger than a few nucleons across, each nucleon only feels its near neighbours: the attraction per nucleon levels off.', 'Electrostatic repulsion acts across the whole nucleus and keeps growing as protons are added.', 'So beyond about iron, repulsion lowers the binding per nucleon: the peak is where these effects balance.']) },
  { id: 'p8be06', dp: '8.4.6', t: 'calc', q: 'Calculate the binding energy of a tritium atom. Masses: proton 1.007276 u, neutron 1.008665 u, electron 0.000549 u, tritium atom 3.016049 u. Give the binding energy per nucleon.',
    a: OL(['Components: 1 proton + 2 neutrons + 1 electron = 1.007276 + 2(1.008665) + 0.000549 = 3.025155 u', 'Mass defect = 3.025155 − 3.016049 = 0.009106 u', 'E = 0.009106 × 931.5 = <b>8.48 MeV</b>', 'Per nucleon: 8.48 ÷ 3 = <b>2.83 MeV per nucleon</b>']) + N('Compare like with like: atomic mass with the electron included in the components (as here), or nuclear mass with no electron.') },
  { id: 'p8be07', dp: '8.4.6', t: 'calc', q: 'The binding energy of nickel-62 is 545.281 MeV. Find its binding energy per nucleon in MeV and in joules. What is the total binding energy of U-235 (7.59 MeV per nucleon)?',
    a: L(['Ni-62: 545.281 ÷ 62 = <b>8.79 MeV per nucleon</b> = 8.79 × 10^6 × 1.602 × 10^{-19} = <b>1.41 × 10^{-12} J</b>', 'U-235: 7.59 × 235 = <b>1784 MeV</b>', 'Ni-62 has the higher binding energy per nucleon, so it is more stable, even though U-235 has the larger total binding energy.']) },
  { id: 'p8be08', dp: '8.4.6', t: 'calc', q: `NESA 2013 (3 marks): Calculate the energy released in ${NU(235, 92, 'U')} + ${NE} → ${NU(141, 56, 'Ba')} + ${NU(92, 36, 'Kr')} + 3 ${NE}. Masses (u): U-235 235.0439, Ba-141 140.9144, Kr-92 91.9263, n 1.008665.`,
    a: OL(['Reactants: 235.0439 + 1.008665 = 236.0526 u', 'Products: 140.9144 + 91.9263 + 3(1.008665) = 235.8667 u', 'Mass defect: 236.0526 − 235.8667 = 0.1859 u', 'E = 0.1859 × 931.5 = <b>173 MeV</b> (2.78 × 10^{-11} J)']) + N('Your booklet’s answer page gives 194.86 MeV because its products total is added incorrectly (235.8433 instead of 235.8667 u). 173 MeV is correct.') },
  { id: 'p8be09', dp: '8.4.6', t: 'calc', q: 'NESA 2015 (3 marks): Polonium-218 (218.00897 u) alpha decays to lead-214 (213.99981 u). The alpha particle is 4.00260 u. Calculate the energy released.',
    a: E(`${NU(218, 84, 'Po')} → ${NU(214, 82, 'Pb')} + ${AL}`) + OL(['Products: 213.99981 + 4.00260 = 218.00241 u', 'Mass defect: 218.00897 − 218.00241 = 0.00656 u', 'E = 0.00656 × 931.5 = <b>6.11 MeV</b>, or E = mc^2 = 0.00656 × 1.661 × 10^{-27} × (3.00 × 10^8)^2 = <b>9.8 × 10^{-13} J</b>']) },
  { id: 'p8be10', dp: '8.4.6', t: 'calc', q: 'Potassium-42 (41.962417 u) undergoes β⁻ decay to calcium-42 (41.958628 u). Write the equation and find the energy released. These are atomic masses.',
    a: E(`${NU(42, 19, 'K')} → ${NU(42, 20, 'Ca')} + ${BM} + ${NB}`) + OL(['Mass defect = 41.962417 − 41.958628 = 0.003789 u', 'E = 0.003789 × 931.5 = <b>3.53 MeV</b>']) + N('Atomic masses already include the electrons: the calcium atom has one more electron than the potassium atom, which accounts for the emitted electron. Your booklet’s worked answer also adds an electron mass to the products and gets 3.02 MeV; that counts the electron twice.') },
  { id: 'p8be11', dp: '8.4.6', t: 'calc', q: 'In a fission bomb 0.1% of the mass of fissioning uranium becomes energy. If 0.7 kg undergoes fission, how much mass is converted and how much energy is released?',
    a: L(['Mass converted = 0.1% × 0.7 kg = 7 × 10^{-4} kg = <b>0.7 g</b>', 'E = mc^2 = 7 × 10^{-4} × (3.00 × 10^8)^2 = <b>6.3 × 10^{13} J</b>']) },
  { id: 'p8be12', dp: '8.4.4', t: 'exam', q: 'NESA 2012 (3 marks): How does the equivalence between mass and energy relate to what occurs during natural radioactive decay?',
    a: L(['Decay happens spontaneously only if the mass of the parent nucleus is <b>greater</b> than the combined mass of the daughter nucleus and the emitted particles.', 'The mass defect is converted into energy (E = Δmc^2).', 'This energy is carried away as kinetic energy of the emitted α or β particle and the recoiling daughter nucleus, and as gamma radiation.']) + N('If the products had more mass than the reactants, energy would have to be supplied, so the decay could not happen naturally.') },
  { id: 'p8be13', dp: '8.4.4', t: 'con', q: 'How did the energy spectrum of beta particles lead to the prediction of the neutrino?',
    a: L(['Experiments showed that beta particles are emitted with a <b>continuous range</b> of kinetic energies, not one fixed value.', 'If only an electron were emitted, every decay would give the same energy, so energy appeared to be lost, breaking conservation of energy.', '<b>Pauli</b> proposed that a very light, neutral particle is emitted too, sharing the energy with the electron, so the total is always the same.', 'Fermi named it the neutrino and included it in his theory of beta decay (1934). In β⁻ decay it is actually an antineutrino.']) },
  { id: 'p8be14', dp: '8.4.5', t: 'con', q: 'Account for the release of energy in nuclear fusion. Why is fusion difficult to achieve on Earth?',
    a: L(['When light nuclei fuse, the product has <b>less mass</b> than the reactants; the mass defect becomes energy (E = Δmc^2). The product has a higher binding energy per nucleon.', 'The positive nuclei strongly <b>repel</b> each other, so they need very high speeds (extremely high temperature) and density to get close enough for the strong force to act.', 'In the Sun, gravity provides the pressure, and the core is about 15 million °C; the Sun converts about 600 million tonnes of hydrogen to helium each second.', 'On Earth, the deuterium–tritium reaction needs about 150 million °C. Projects such as ITER struggle to get more energy out than is put in.']) },
  { id: 'p8be15', dp: '8.4.4', t: 'exam', q: 'Distinguish between nuclear fission and transmutation.',
    a: L(['<b>Transmutation</b>: any change of one element into another, by radioactive decay or by bombardment (e.g. nitrogen to oxygen by alpha particles).', '<b>Fission</b>: a specific nuclear reaction in which a heavy nucleus absorbs a neutron and splits into two smaller nuclei of similar size, releasing neutrons and a large amount of energy.']) }
]});

/* ================= Inquiry Question 8.5: Deep Inside the Atom ================= */
DECKS.push({ id: 'phys-m8-pa', fa: 'fa5', title: 'New particles and particle accelerators', dps: ['8.5.1', '8.5.3'], cards: [
  { id: 'p8pa01', dp: '8.5.1', t: 'def', q: 'Define <b>elementary particle</b>, <b>composite particle</b>, <b>hadron</b>, <b>lepton</b> and <b>quark</b>.',
    a: L(['<b>Elementary (fundamental) particle</b>: not made of smaller particles.', '<b>Composite particle</b>: made of two or more elementary particles.', '<b>Hadron</b>: a heavy composite particle made of quarks, affected by the strong force (e.g. protons, neutrons).', '<b>Lepton</b>: an elementary particle not affected by the strong force (e.g. the electron, neutrinos).', '<b>Quark</b>: an elementary particle that builds hadrons; quarks have fractional charge and feel the strong force.']) },
  { id: 'p8pa02', dp: '8.5.1', t: 'con', q: 'How was the <b>positron</b> discovered (1932), and how was it identified?',
    a: L(['It was observed in a <b>cloud chamber</b> after a <b>cosmic ray</b> collision.', 'A magnetic field across the chamber curved its track with the <b>same radius as an electron’s</b> (same mass and size of charge) but in the <b>opposite direction</b> (positive charge).', 'It is the antiparticle of the electron. When a positron meets an electron they annihilate, and their mass becomes energy (gamma rays), E = mc^2.']) },
  { id: 'p8pa03', dp: '8.5.1', t: 'prac', q: 'How does a <b>cloud chamber</b> detect charged particles?',
    a: L(['It contains air supersaturated with water or alcohol vapour.', 'A charged particle passing through <b>ionises</b> the vapour, leaving a trail of ions.', 'The vapour condenses on the ions, forming a visible line of droplets along the particle’s path.', 'A magnetic field curves the tracks, showing the sign of the charge and, from the radius, information about mass and speed. Alpha particles leave short, thick tracks; beta particles thin, longer ones.']) },
  { id: 'p8pa04', dp: '8.5.1', t: 'con', q: 'How did cosmic ray experiments and accelerators show there were more subatomic particles than protons, neutrons and electrons?',
    a: L(['<b>Cosmic rays</b> (very high-energy particles, mostly protons, from the Sun and other stars) collide with the upper atmosphere; energy becomes mass (E = mc^2), creating new particles.', 'In the 1930s–40s, experiments on mountains and in aircraft found the <b>muon</b>, pions (mesons) and kaons: short-lived particles that no atomic or nuclear model could explain.', 'In the 1950s–60s, more powerful <b>particle accelerators</b> produced over 150 new hadrons (the ‘particle zoo’), at first thought to be elementary.', 'These were later explained as combinations of a few elementary particles: <b>quarks</b>.']) + N('Your booklet dates the muon to 1912 in one place; it was discovered in cosmic rays in 1936.') },
  { id: 'p8pa05', dp: '8.5.1', t: 'con', q: 'What is the evidence that protons are not fundamental particles?',
    a: L(['In <b>1968 at the Stanford Linear Accelerator Center (SLAC)</b>, very high-energy electrons were fired at protons (and neutrons) in nuclei.', 'Electrons can be accelerated to high energies and are point-like, so they probe inside the proton (like Rutherford’s alpha particles probing the atom).', 'The <b>scattering pattern</b> (large-angle, inelastic scattering) showed the proton contains <b>three much smaller, point-like objects</b>, later identified as up and down quarks.', 'So protons have internal structure (uud) and are <b>composite</b>. Later SLAC experiments also gave evidence for the strange quark.']) },
  { id: 'p8pa06', dp: '8.5.1', t: 'con', q: 'What is the evidence that neutrons are not fundamental particles?',
    a: L(['<b>Magnetic moment</b>: nuclear magnetic resonance measurements showed the neutron has a magnetic field, even though it has no overall charge. This strongly implies moving charges inside it.', '<b>Beta decay</b>: a neutron can change into a proton, emitting an electron and an antineutrino. Since there are no electrons in the nucleus, the neutron must have an internal structure that can change (a down quark becoming an up quark: udd → uud).', 'Electron scattering (SLAC) also showed internal structure in neutrons.']) },
  { id: 'p8pa07', dp: '8.5.3', t: 'con', q: 'Explain how a <b>linear accelerator</b> works, including why the drift tubes get longer.',
    a: L(['Charged particles pass through a line of hollow cylindrical electrodes (drift tubes) connected to an <b>alternating</b> voltage.', 'In each <b>gap</b> between tubes an electric field (E = V/d) exerts a force F = qE that accelerates the particle. Inside a tube there is no field, so it moves at constant speed.', 'While the particle is inside a tube, the polarity <b>switches</b>, so at the next gap it is again pushed from behind and pulled from in front.', 'Its speed increases at every gap, so it travels further in each half-cycle: each tube must be <b>longer</b> so that the particle reaches each gap exactly as the polarity switches.']) + N('SLAC’s linear accelerator is 3.2 km long.') },
  { id: 'p8pa08', dp: '8.5.3', t: 'con', q: 'Explain how a <b>cyclotron</b> works.',
    a: L(['Two hollow D-shaped electrodes (‘dees’) sit in a uniform <b>magnetic field</b> perpendicular to them, in a vacuum.', 'The magnetic force (qvB = mv^2/r) makes particles move in semicircles inside the dees.', 'A high-frequency <b>alternating voltage</b> across the gap accelerates the particles (F = qE) each time they cross it.', 'As they speed up, the radius (r = mv/qB) increases, so they <b>spiral outwards</b> until they reach the edge and are deflected to the target. Particles come out continuously.']) },
  { id: 'p8pa09', dp: '8.5.3', t: 'con', q: 'How does a <b>synchrotron</b> work, and what is its advantage and disadvantage compared with a cyclotron?',
    a: L(['Particles (pre-accelerated, e.g. by a linear accelerator) travel around a <b>ring of fixed radius</b>.', 'Electric fields in accelerating cavities around the ring increase their speed each lap.', 'The <b>magnetic field is increased</b> as they speed up, so the radius (r = mv/qB) stays <b>constant</b>.', '<b>Advantage</b>: reaches much higher energies (only a narrow ring of magnets is needed, not a huge solid magnet).', '<b>Disadvantage</b>: accelerates only one packet of particles at a time; a cyclotron emits particles continuously.']) },
  { id: 'p8pa10', dp: '8.5.3', t: 'rec', q: 'Describe the <b>Large Hadron Collider</b> (LHC).',
    a: L(['The world’s largest and most powerful particle accelerator, at CERN; first started on 10 September 2008.', 'A <b>27 km synchrotron</b> ring of superconducting magnets.', 'Two beams of protons (or lead ions) travel in opposite directions in separate ultrahigh-vacuum pipes at close to the speed of light, then collide.', 'Its superconducting electromagnets are cooled by liquid helium to −271.3 °C.', 'Three essential parts: the accelerator, the <b>detectors</b> (in four huge chambers around the ring) and the <b>Grid</b> (a global computer network that processes the data).']) },
  { id: 'p8pa11', dp: '8.5.3', t: 'con', q: 'Why did superconducting electromagnets allow accelerator physics to advance?',
    a: L(['Below their critical temperature they have no electrical resistance, so very large currents flow without heating or energy loss.', 'This produces much <b>stronger magnetic fields</b>, which can keep faster, more energetic particles in a ring (r = mv/qB).', 'So higher collision energies can be reached, creating more massive particles.']) },
  { id: 'p8pa12', dp: '8.5.3', t: 'exam', q: '2004 HSC (7 marks): Describe how the key features and components of the Standard Model of matter have been developed using accelerators as a probe. Outline the key points.',
    a: L(['Accelerators use electric fields (F = qE) to give charged particles very high energies, and magnetic fields (qvB = mv^2/r) to steer them.', '<b>Probing structure</b>: SLAC fired high-energy electrons at protons and neutrons; the scattering showed three point-like quarks inside each, so nucleons are composite (uud, udd). Further SLAC experiments found evidence for the strange quark.', '<b>Creating particles</b>: in high-energy collisions, kinetic energy becomes mass (E = mc^2), producing heavier, short-lived particles, such as the later-generation quarks and leptons.', 'Organising these results gave the <b>Standard Model</b>: 6 quarks, 6 leptons and force-carrying bosons.', '<b>Testing it</b>: the Standard Model predicted the Higgs boson, which the LHC found in 2012.']) }
]});

DECKS.push({ id: 'phys-m8-sm', fa: 'fa5', title: 'The Standard Model of matter', dps: ['8.5.2', '8.5.3'], cards: [
  { id: 'p8sm01', dp: '8.5.2', t: 'rec', q: 'Outline the key features and components of the Standard Model of matter.',
    a: L(['All matter is made of <b>12 fundamental particles</b> (fermions): <b>6 quarks</b> (up, down, charm, strange, top, bottom) and <b>6 leptons</b> (electron, muon, tau and their three neutrinos), each with an antiparticle.', 'Quarks combine to form <b>hadrons</b>: baryons (3 quarks, e.g. proton uud, neutron udd) and mesons (a quark and an antiquark).', 'Forces are carried by <b>bosons</b>: gluons (strong), photons (electromagnetic), W and Z bosons (weak). The graviton (gravity) is proposed but not observed.', 'The <b>Higgs boson</b> is linked to the field that gives particles mass.']), v: 'p8quarks' },
  { id: 'p8sm02', dp: '8.5.2', t: 'con', q: 'Distinguish <b>fermions</b> from <b>bosons</b>.',
    a: L(['<b>Fermions</b>: the matter particles (quarks and leptons, and hadrons made from quarks). They have <b>half-integer spin</b> and obey the Pauli exclusion principle.', '<b>Bosons</b>: force-carrying particles (gluon, photon, W, Z) plus the Higgs boson. They have <b>integer spin</b> (0, 1, 2) and do not obey the Pauli exclusion principle.']) },
  { id: 'p8sm03', dp: '8.5.2', t: 'rec', q: 'Describe quarks: their flavours, charges and properties.',
    a: L(['Six flavours in three generations: up (+⅔e) and down (−⅓e); charm (+⅔e) and strange (−⅓e); top (+⅔e) and bottom (−⅓e).', 'The only known particles with <b>fractional charge</b>.', 'They <b>cannot exist on their own</b>; they are always bound in hadrons.', 'Held together by the strong force, carried by <b>gluons</b>.', '1st-generation quarks (u, d) form stable matter; 2nd and 3rd generations form unstable particles seen only in high-energy situations (cosmic rays, accelerators, the early Universe). The top quark is very massive.']) },
  { id: 'p8sm04', dp: '8.5.2', t: 'con', q: 'Show how the quark compositions of the proton and neutron give their charges.',
    a: L(['<b>Proton = uud</b>: +⅔ + ⅔ − ⅓ = <b>+1</b>', '<b>Neutron = udd</b>: +⅔ − ⅓ − ⅓ = <b>0</b>']) + N('Memory trick: ‘neUtron’ has only one u, so it is udd.') },
  { id: 'p8sm05', dp: '8.5.2', t: 'exam', q: '2004 HSC (2 marks): Baryons have three quarks; mesons such as the pions π⁺ and π⁻ have one quark and one antiquark. Using only up and down quarks and their antiquarks, state the quark composition of the neutron and the negative pion.',
    a: L(['<b>Neutron: udd</b> (one up, two down): +⅔ − ⅓ − ⅓ = 0', '<b>π⁻: d + anti-up</b> (written dū): −⅓ + (−⅔) = −1. This is the only quark–antiquark pair from u and d that gives −1.']) },
  { id: 'p8sm06', dp: '8.5.2', t: 'rec', q: 'Describe <b>leptons</b>.',
    a: L(['Six leptons: <b>electron, muon, tau</b> (each charge −1) and the <b>electron neutrino, muon neutrino and tau neutrino</b> (neutral, with tiny mass); each has an antiparticle.', '<b>Fundamental</b>: they can’t be split up, and can exist on their own.', 'They do <b>not</b> feel the strong force; they interact by the electromagnetic and/or weak force.', 'Only the electron and the electron neutrino are found in normal matter.']) },
  { id: 'p8sm07', dp: '8.5.2', t: 'rec', q: 'Compare the four fundamental forces: what each acts on, its carrier, range and relative strength.',
    a: P('In order of strength: strong, electromagnetic, weak, gravitational.'), v: 'p8forces4' },
  { id: 'p8sm08', dp: '8.5.2', t: 'con', q: 'How does the Standard Model explain β⁻ decay?',
    a: L(['A neutron (udd) changes into a proton (uud): one <b>down quark changes into an up quark</b>.', 'This is mediated by the <b>weak force</b>: the down quark emits a <b>W⁻ boson</b>.', 'The W⁻ boson quickly decays into an <b>electron</b> and an <b>antineutrino</b>.', 'This explains where the electron comes from even though there are no electrons in the nucleus: an example of the Standard Model explaining experimental evidence.']) + E(`${NE} → ${NU(1, 1, 'p')} + ${BM} + ${NB}`) },
  { id: 'p8sm09', dp: '8.5.2', t: 'con', q: 'What is the <b>Higgs boson</b>, and why was its discovery a success for the Standard Model?',
    a: L(['The Standard Model did not explain why particles have mass. The <b>Higgs mechanism</b> proposed that space is filled with a <b>Higgs field</b>; particles get their mass by interacting with it (strong interaction = heavy, weak interaction = light).', 'The field should have an associated particle, the Higgs boson.', 'CERN announced evidence for it at the <b>LHC in 2012</b>: a prediction of the model confirmed by experiment.']) },
  { id: 'p8sm10', dp: '8.5.2', t: 'rec', q: 'What are the limitations of, and unanswered questions in, the Standard Model?',
    a: L(['It does not include <b>gravity</b>: the graviton has never been observed, and there is no unified theory of all four forces (a goal of a GUT or ‘Theory of Everything’; supersymmetry is one proposal).', 'Visible matter is only about 4% of the Universe; <b>dark matter</b> (about 23%) and <b>dark energy</b> (about 73%) are unexplained.', 'It does not explain why the Universe is made almost entirely of <b>matter</b> when the Big Bang should have made equal amounts of matter and antimatter.', 'It is a theory, so it could change as new evidence (e.g. from the LHC, such as quark–gluon plasma studies) appears.']) },
  { id: 'p8sm11', dp: '8.5.2', t: 'exam', q: '2009 HSC (6 marks): “Theories and experiments not only help increase our understanding but also generate new questions.” Use the Standard Model of matter to support this statement.',
    a: L(['<b>Increased understanding</b>: experiments showed the nucleus is made of protons and neutrons (Rutherford, Chadwick). Then cosmic ray and accelerator experiments found many new particles (muons, mesons, the ‘particle zoo’), and SLAC showed protons and neutrons contain quarks.', 'The Standard Model organised all of this: 6 quarks, 6 leptons and force-carrying bosons, explaining hadrons and β decay, and predicting particles (e.g. the Higgs boson, found in 2012).', '<b>New questions</b>: why is gravity not included, and does the graviton exist? What are dark matter and dark energy? Why is there more matter than antimatter? Why do particles have the masses they do?', 'So each advance answered old questions but raised new ones, driving further experiments such as those at the LHC.']) },
  { id: 'p8sm12', dp: '8.5.2', t: 'con', q: 'Why were the up and down quarks, the electron and the electron neutrino the first fundamental particles discovered?',
    a: L(['They have the <b>smallest masses</b>, and they make up ordinary, stable matter.', 'Heavier particles need more energy to create in a collision (m = E/c^2), and they are short-lived.', 'So the 2nd- and 3rd-generation particles were only found once particle accelerators could reach high enough energies.']) },
  { id: 'p8sm13', dp: '8.5.3', t: 'exam', q: '2019 HSC Q32 (5 marks): Describe how specific experiments have contributed to our understanding of the electron and ONE other fundamental particle.',
    a: L(['<b>Electron</b>: Thomson balanced electric and magnetic forces on cathode rays to find their charge-to-mass ratio, showing they are tiny negative particles in all atoms. Millikan balanced electric and gravitational forces on charged oil drops and found charge comes in multiples of 1.6 × 10^{-19} C, the electron’s charge.', '<b>Quarks</b>: at SLAC (1968), high-energy electrons were fired at protons. The scattering pattern showed each proton contains three smaller point-like particles, later identified as up and down quarks, so protons are not fundamental.']) + N('Other answers could use the wave properties of electrons (Davisson and Germer) or the discovery of heavier quarks in accelerators.') }
]});

HSC.addModule({ id: 'phys-m8', focus: FOCUS, dotPoints: DOTS, decks: DECKS, viz: VIZ });
